  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You finish a task and upload the file, but the next person says the required naming convention is wrong and their system cannot process it. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Rename and re-upload it correctly, confirm the handoff works, and then close the task.$qb$, $qb$Correct the filename and tell the next person it is available.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the contents are correct and ask them to rename it.$qb$, $qb$Mark your work complete because only the filename is wrong.$qb$, $qb$Wait for someone else to fix the handoff.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You finish a task, but one promised attachment is missing from the final email. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Send the complete package before marking the task finished$qb$, $qb$Add the attachment immediately and notify the recipient$qb$, $qb$Mark the task complete and send the attachment later$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether the recipient notices$qb$, $qb$Leave it out because the main work is finished$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You complete a report and send it, but the recipient cannot open the attachment. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Send a working file and confirm they can access it before closing the task$qb$, $qb$Resend the file in another format$qb$, $qb$Tell them the report itself is complete$qb$, $qb$Ask them to contact IT$qb$, $qb$Mark the task complete because you sent the report$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate says your delivered file is correct but asks one final question needed to use it. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Answer the question and confirm they can proceed before closing the handoff$qb$, $qb$Answer the question promptly$qb$, $qb$Tell them the file itself is complete$qb$, $qb$Ask them to work it out from the file$qb$, $qb$Close the handoff because delivery is complete$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You finish a task but notice the tracking system still shows the old status. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Update the status accurately and make sure any dependent person can see the completion$qb$, $qb$Update the tracker before moving to the next task$qb$, $qb$Leave it because the actual work is complete$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to update the tracker$qb$, $qb$Wait until the weekly status review$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You finish a task and the output is correct, but the agreed evidence has not been attached. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Attach the evidence and verify the complete deliverable before closing$qb$, $qb$Add the evidence before updating the task status$qb$, $qb$Close the task and attach the evidence later$qb$, $qb$Tell the recipient the evidence is available if needed$qb$, $qb$Close it because the output itself is correct$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have finished a request, but the person who raised it has not confirmed whether the result works. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to verify the result and close the request after confirmation or agreed acceptance criteria$qb$, $qb$Send a completion message and ask them to confirm$qb$, $qb$Close it because the work is finished$qb$, $qb$Leave it open forever until they reply$qb$, $qb$Delete the request from your list$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A transformation has delivered the promised system, but adoption is uneven and old workarounds remain active. The program sponsor wants closure this month. When is closure justified?$qb$, $qb$When operating ownership, adoption, controls and retirement of critical workarounds meet agreed acceptance criteria, not merely when the system is live.$qb$, $qb$When the sponsor accepts the system and the remaining adoption actions have owners.$qb$, $qb$When more than half of users have moved to the new process.$qb$, $qb$When the implementation team has completed its contracted deliverables.$qb$, $qb$When the original project budget has been fully spent.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A program is ready to disband, but a supplier dispute and two control exceptions remain unresolved. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Determine whether each residual issue has a capable permanent owner, clear authority, funding and governance; transfer them formally before closing the temporary program.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$At first, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$In this case, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You complete a change requested by a colleague. The receiving person cannot progress until the handoff is genuinely usable. What best confirms the work is truly closed?$qb$, $qb$The colleague confirms the change meets the agreed need$qb$, $qb$You verify the change and tell the colleague it is complete$qb$, $qb$The change is saved in the system$qb$, $qb$You mark the task as done in your tracker$qb$, $qb$You stop working on it because the change was made$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A deliverable is technically complete, but two users have not adopted the change and the expected result is not yet visible. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the agreed completion criteria, address remaining adoption actions, and close only when the intended outcome is reasonably secured$qb$, $qb$Follow up with the two users and confirm what prevents adoption$qb$, $qb$Close the technical work and create a separate adoption task$qb$, $qb$Mark it complete because the deliverable was produced$qb$, $qb$Wait indefinitely for every user to confirm success$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A change is deployed and users can technically use it, but support tickets show repeated confusion. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Treat the adoption issue as unfinished outcome work, address the confusion, and confirm the intended result before declaring closure$qb$, $qb$Review the support tickets and provide targeted guidance$qb$, $qb$Close the implementation and create a separate support activity$qb$, $qb$Wait for ticket volume to fall on its own$qb$, $qb$Remove the change because users are confused$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R2-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Which meeting action is the clearest?$qb$, $qb$Meera to send the approved vendor list by 4 PM Thursday.$qb$, $qb$Meera to send the approved vendor list by Thursday.$qb$, $qb$Vendor list to be sent soon.$qb$, $qb$Meera will follow up on the vendor list.$qb$, $qb$Vendor list discussed; action to be confirmed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Which handover note is the most precise?$qb$, $qb$Client login restored; password reset confirmation still pending with Support.$qb$, $qb$Client issue resolved except for password confirmation.$qb$, $qb$Client issue almost complete; one step remains.$qb$, $qb$Client issue improved after support action.$qb$, $qb$Client issue handled.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Which action note is easiest for another person to follow?$qb$, $qb$Asha to confirm vendor quantity by 1 PM Wednesday and update the order sheet$qb$, $qb$Asha to confirm vendor quantity by Wednesday$qb$, $qb$Vendor quantity to be confirmed soon$qb$, $qb$Asha to check vendor details$qb$, $qb$Vendor issue discussed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Which record is clearest after a customer call?$qb$, $qb$Customer approved the revised quantity; Omar to send the updated order by 4 PM Thursday$qb$, $qb$Customer approved the revised quantity; updated order due Thursday$qb$, $qb$Customer agreed; order update to follow$qb$, $qb$Call completed; order needs update$qb$, $qb$Customer call done$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Which note gives the clearest record of a changed deadline?$qb$, $qb$Deadline moved from 18 June to 20 June after vendor delay; client informed at 3 PM$qb$, $qb$Deadline moved to 20 June because of vendor delay$qb$, $qb$Deadline changed due to vendor issue$qb$, $qb$New deadline: 20 June$qb$, $qb$Schedule updated$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Which handover note gives the next person the clearest instruction?$qb$, $qb$Invoice checked; Rohan to confirm tax code by 10 AM, then Meera can submit payment$qb$, $qb$Invoice checked; tax code confirmation needed by 10 AM$qb$, $qb$Invoice mostly ready; tax code still open$qb$, $qb$Please check the tax code before payment$qb$, $qb$Invoice handover complete$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Which note best records a decision made in a meeting?$qb$, $qb$Decision: use Vendor B; approved by Kavita on 8 July; Arjun to issue the order by noon tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Vendor B selected; Arjun to issue the order tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Team agreed to use Vendor B$qb$, $qb$Vendor discussion completed$qb$, $qb$Decision made$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is essential when creating standard operating procedures (SOPs) for junior staff executing complex tasks?$qb$, $qb$Specifying step-by-step instructions with visual aids, explicit edge-case rules, and user-testing validation.$qb$, $qb$Writing a detailed narrative explaining the background, philosophy, and strategic goals of the operational task.$qb$, $qb$Providing bulleted task guidelines while encouraging staff to seek verbal guidance whenever edge cases arise.$qb$, $qb$Recording an unscripted video walkthrough of the task and uploading it as the primary operational reference.$qb$, $qb$Adapting generic department guides by adding brief summary notes specific to the new operational task.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A critical decision memo cites 'acceptable risk' without saying who decides what acceptable means. What should it specify?$qb$, $qb$Define thresholds, required evidence, accountable decision owner, escalation path and treatment of exceptions.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global standard says exceptions require 'senior approval,' causing different thresholds by country. What should be clarified?$qb$, $qb$Specify the accountable role or authority level, evidence required, decision criteria, recording and escalation rules rather than relying on an ambiguous seniority label.$qb$, $qb$At first, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$In this case, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-020$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have accumulated responsibilities because people trust your judgment, but the organization is now slower whenever you are unavailable. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Convert personal judgment into institutional principles, talent and decision systems, delegate visibly and measure success by the organization's ability to decide well without you.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The record may be relied on later by someone not present. Which status note gives the most useful record of a delayed task?$qb$, $qb$Testing delayed to Wednesday because test data arrived late; Meera owns the retest$qb$, $qb$Testing delayed to Wednesday because data arrived late$qb$, $qb$Testing delayed; retest planned for Wednesday$qb$, $qb$Testing is taking longer than expected$qb$, $qb$Testing delayed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are documenting a decision that involved several options and a trade-off. Which record is most useful?$qb$, $qb$Decision, date, owner, chosen option, key reason, important trade-off, and next action$qb$, $qb$Decision, owner, chosen option, and main reason$qb$, $qb$Chosen option and a short note on why it was selected$qb$, $qb$Decision and date only$qb$, $qb$A note saying the options were discussed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A decision has financial impact and was made after considering two acceptable alternatives. Which record is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Record the chosen option, date, owner, evidence, material alternatives considered, rationale, impact, and follow-up action$qb$, $qb$Record the chosen option, main alternatives, owner, and rationale$qb$, $qb$Record the final decision and the expected financial impact$qb$, $qb$Record the decision and who approved it$qb$, $qb$Keep the supporting discussion in meeting notes only$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your task depends on information from another team next week. What should you do now?$qb$, $qb$Confirm what you need, who will provide it, and by when$qb$, $qb$Send the other team an early reminder about the information$qb$, $qb$Add extra time to your own plan in case the input is late$qb$, $qb$Wait until the day before you need the information$qb$, $qb$Start the dependent work without confirming the input$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R2-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A task next week depends on another person's input. What should you do now?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the exact input, owner, and needed date early enough to protect the schedule.$qb$, $qb$Send an early reminder about the information you will need.$qb$, $qb$Add extra time to your own plan in case the input is late.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the day before you need the input.$qb$, $qb$Start dependent work without confirming the requirement.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have a task due Friday and need approval from someone who is away on Thursday. What is the best plan?$qb$, $qb$Seek the approval early enough to avoid the Thursday dependency$qb$, $qb$Send the approval request on Wednesday with the deadline stated$qb$, $qb$Prepare everything else and request approval on Thursday$qb$, $qb$Wait until Friday morning because the task is due Friday$qb$, $qb$Submit without approval if the approver is unavailable$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You know a holiday next week will reduce the time available for a deadline. What is the best plan?$qb$, $qb$Move key work earlier and confirm any dependencies before the holiday$qb$, $qb$Adjust your schedule now and warn others if their input is needed earlier$qb$, $qb$Plan to work faster after the holiday$qb$, $qb$Wait until the holiday is over to see what remains$qb$, $qb$Keep the original plan without adjustment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A project starts Monday and requires system access that can take three days to approve. What is the best action?$qb$, $qb$Request access before the project starts and confirm it is ready in time$qb$, $qb$Submit the access request early and track its status$qb$, $qb$Request access on Monday morning$qb$, $qb$Plan work that does not need access for the first three days$qb$, $qb$Assume access will be approved quickly$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A deliverable due Friday needs two reviews, and each reviewer usually takes a day. What is the best plan?$qb$, $qb$Work backward from Friday and schedule both reviews with enough time for changes$qb$, $qb$Send the first reviewer an early draft and reserve time for the second review$qb$, $qb$Finish the deliverable Thursday and request both reviews$qb$, $qb$Ask both reviewers to review it at the same time on Friday$qb$, $qb$Ignore review time when planning$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Which action best represents proactive planning when preparing for an anticipated 40% surge in seasonal customer tickets?$qb$, $qb$Forecasting operational resource requirements, drafting contingency schedules, and presenting capacity plans early.$qb$, $qb$Monitoring ticket queues weekly and hiring temporary staff once daily ticket limits exceed standard capacity.$qb$, $qb$Requesting emergency overtime budget authorization starting the week the seasonal surge begins.$qb$, $qb$Advising service agents to shorten individual response lengths during peak weeks to handle higher volume.$qb$, $qb$Maintaining current staffing levels until ticket surges occur to evaluate actual workload demand.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your three-year plan depends heavily on one market assumption that now looks less certain. Which planning approach is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Model plausible scenarios, identify decisions that are robust across them, and define trigger points for choices that can wait.$qb$, $qb$Replace the assumption with the latest forecast and rebuild the plan around it.$qb$, $qb$Keep the approved plan but add a larger contingency reserve.$qb$, $qb$Ask each function to create its own scenario plan independently.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing the plan until the assumption is proven wrong.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are setting a five-year investment portfolio where regulation, technology and demand could each shift materially. Which architecture is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Use no-regret moves and strategic options with scenario-linked triggers, capital reallocation rules and periodic assumption reviews.$qb$, $qb$Use one base-case plan with a large central contingency reserve.$qb$, $qb$Build three fixed five-year budgets for optimistic, base and pessimistic cases.$qb$, $qb$Let departments revise annual budgets when conditions change.$qb$, $qb$Use a conservative plan based on the weakest plausible outcome.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A ten-year strategy depends on cheap energy, but policy and technology could radically alter costs. What planning approach is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Make the dependency explicit, invest in no-regret resilience, develop options for divergent futures, and define signals that trigger capital shifts.$qb$, $qb$Take a practical partial step and escalate the remaining uncertainty to a higher authority.$qb$, $qb$Apply the existing policy more carefully, even though it does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects the most visible short-term target.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the conflicting evidence and continue with the established position.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy assumes regulatory approval within two years, but timing is outside your control and capital commitments begin now. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Stage commitments around approval-dependent milestones, preserve alternative uses of capital, and define triggers to accelerate, redirect or stop.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A five-year capacity plan depends on one customer segment continuing rapid growth, but leading indicators are diverging. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Use scenarios and staged capacity commitments, define demand signals for expansion or restraint, and preserve options where irreversible investment is greatest.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic investment depends on commodity prices remaining within a wide range for seven years. What is the strongest planning response?$qb$, $qb$Model threshold economics, hedge or stage exposure where sensible, and define price signals that trigger changes rather than relying on one long-term forecast.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$At first, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$In this case, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-018$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A decade-long transformation is ending, but some benefits still depend on the personal sponsorship of the current CEO. What should happen?$qb$, $qb$Institutionalize the incentives, decision rights, measures and leadership routines that sustain the benefits, test them without CEO intervention and only then treat the transformation as closed.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A task next month depends on a supplier response that often takes a week. A delay would reduce time available for later review. What is the best action now?$qb$, $qb$Request the information early and include time for follow-up in the plan$qb$, $qb$Contact the supplier now and state when the response is needed$qb$, $qb$Add a week to the final deadline without contacting the supplier$qb$, $qb$Wait until the task officially starts$qb$, $qb$Assume the supplier will respond faster this time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A project due in four weeks depends on two approvals and an external input with uncertain timing. What is the best plan?$qb$, $qb$Map the dependencies, secure early commitments, set follow-up points, and protect time for likely delays$qb$, $qb$Request the approvals and external input early and track them closely$qb$, $qb$Add extra time at the end of the project$qb$, $qb$Focus on internal work first and deal with dependencies later$qb$, $qb$Assume normal turnaround times and adjust only if something slips$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A project has a fixed delivery date, but two external dependencies have uncertain timing and limited alternatives. What is the best plan?$qb$, $qb$Identify critical paths, secure commitments early, define contingency actions, and monitor trigger points before the uncertainty becomes a delay$qb$, $qb$Request both dependencies early and set regular follow-ups$qb$, $qb$Add a large buffer at the end of the project$qb$, $qb$Focus only on tasks the team controls$qb$, $qb$Wait until a dependency slips before creating a contingency$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A usual tool is unavailable, but the task can be done safely another way. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Use a safe approved alternative and keep the work moving$qb$, $qb$Check whether another available tool can meet the need$qb$, $qb$Wait for the usual tool because it is familiar$qb$, $qb$Create an untested shortcut without telling anyone$qb$, $qb$Stop the task without looking for alternatives$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need a piece of information that is not in the usual folder. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Check another reliable source or ask the person most likely to know$qb$, $qb$Search the shared systems using related terms$qb$, $qb$Ask several coworkers at the same time$qb$, $qb$Guess based on an older file$qb$, $qb$Stop the task because the usual source is missing$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague who knows a process is unavailable, but there is a current approved guide. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Use the guide, check any uncertain point, and continue safely$qb$, $qb$Follow the guide and note questions for later$qb$, $qb$Wait until the colleague returns$qb$, $qb$Use your memory of a similar process$qb$, $qb$Create your own steps$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A normal data source is unavailable, but two approved sources together contain the needed information. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Combine the approved information carefully and verify that it answers the need$qb$, $qb$Use both sources and cross-check any overlapping data$qb$, $qb$Wait for the normal source to return$qb$, $qb$Use only one source even if information is missing$qb$, $qb$Estimate the missing information$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A needed report is unavailable, but the raw approved data is accessible. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Use the raw data to produce the needed result carefully and verify the calculation$qb$, $qb$Check whether the required figures can be derived from the raw data$qb$, $qb$Wait for the standard report$qb$, $qb$Use an old report instead$qb$, $qb$Estimate the figures from memory$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A routine request cannot be completed through the normal system because of a temporary outage. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Use an approved fallback process, document what you did, and reconcile it when the system returns$qb$, $qb$Check the approved outage procedure and follow it$qb$, $qb$Wait for the system even if the request becomes late$qb$, $qb$Create your own workaround outside approved tools$qb$, $qb$Tell the requester nothing can be done$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A standard report is delayed, but the person waiting only needs one confirmed figure from it. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Get the figure from an approved source, verify it, and provide it without waiting for the full report$qb$, $qb$Find the figure in another approved source and confirm it matches$qb$, $qb$Tell them to wait for the full report$qb$, $qb$Use last month's figure as an estimate$qb$, $qb$Guess the figure from recent trends$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How can an event lead exercise operational ingenuity when printing budgets are unexpectedly cut by 50%?$qb$, $qb$Pioneering digital-first interactive materials, printing essential sheets only, and framing the shift positively.$qb$, $qb$Cutting optional workbook sections to fit content within reduced printing budget limits.$qb$, $qb$Requesting an emergency budget override from department leadership to print complete workbooks as planned.$qb$, $qb$Borrowing unused printing allocation credits from partner department budgets to cover costs.$qb$, $qb$Canceling physical materials entirely and emailing static documents to participants after event completion.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A plant loses a custom component supplier with eight weeks' notice. Requalifying an identical supplier normally takes four months. What is the most resourceful response?$qb$, $qb$Decompose the requirement, identify acceptable substitutes or design changes, run parallel qualification paths, and protect the highest-value demand first.$qb$, $qb$Pay a premium to the existing supplier to extend supply for four months.$qb$, $qb$Find another supplier offering the same component and begin standard qualification.$qb$, $qb$Build inventory immediately using all remaining supply, regardless of forecast.$qb$, $qb$Pause affected production until an identical supplier is fully qualified.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A critical facility depends on a component with a 12-month lead time. Demand may surge, but committing now risks excess capacity. What is the most resourceful response?$qb$, $qb$Explore staged commitments, alternate specifications, supplier options and demand triggers to preserve upside without an irreversible full bet.$qb$, $qb$Order the maximum projected requirement now.$qb$, $qb$Wait for confirmed demand because unused components waste capital.$qb$, $qb$Ask Sales to eliminate demand uncertainty before Operations acts.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current supplier plan and accept whichever constraint materializes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A critical operation depends on one scarce expert who will retire within a year, with no equivalent internal replacement. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Decompose critical knowledge and decisions, reduce single-person dependency through redesign, deliberate transfer and external options, and test successors.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate issue with a reasonable local action, but leave the broader assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather some additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask the affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or externally forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A production bottleneck cannot be solved by hiring fast enough while demand rises. Which response is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Redesign around the constraint using simplification, cross-training, automation, alternate sourcing and demand prioritization, sequenced by time and risk.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A supply disruption removes a specialized material with no direct substitute. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Decompose the material's required functions, explore alternate designs, specifications, processes and partners, and prioritize uses while options are tested.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A critical supplier's factory is unavailable for months and alternative suppliers cannot meet the exact specification. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Break the specification into essential performance requirements, test design and process alternatives with risk owners, and prioritize scarce supply while substitutes qualify.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$At first, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$In this case, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-022$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A severe loss follows a strategic risk explicitly accepted by you and the board, while public pressure demands individual accountability. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the accepted risk and governance decision, investigate execution objectively, support remediation and learning, and refuse to manufacture blame merely to satisfy external pressure.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A standard template is missing one section needed for your task. The alternative must remain approved and controlled. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Add the needed section in a clear way and tell the template owner about the gap$qb$, $qb$Use a suitable section from another approved template$qb$, $qb$Leave the information out because the template has no place for it$qb$, $qb$Put the information in an unrelated section$qb$, $qb$Create a completely new template without checking existing standards$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A recurring task depends on a manual report that is often late, but the required data exists in approved systems. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Design a reliable way to obtain and verify the needed data without waiting for the manual report$qb$, $qb$Build a repeatable extraction from the approved systems and compare it with the report initially$qb$, $qb$Ask the report owner to send it earlier every time$qb$, $qb$Create your own unofficial data source$qb$, $qb$Keep waiting because the manual report is the established process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A weekly reconciliation requires manual work because two approved systems format the same data differently. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Define the required checks, standardize the data mapping, and build a controlled repeatable reconciliation that preserves auditability$qb$, $qb$Create a repeatable mapping between the two approved formats and validate it against the manual result$qb$, $qb$Ask more people to split the weekly reconciliation$qb$, $qb$Use an unapproved tool that merges the files automatically$qb$, $qb$Continue manually because audit work should not be automated$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You planned to finish a detailed task this morning, but two optional meetings appear on your calendar. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check whether your participation is necessary and protect enough uninterrupted time for the priority task.$qb$, $qb$Attend only the meeting where your input is genuinely useful.$qb$, $qb$Attend both meetings and finish the task later.$qb$, $qb$Multitask on the detailed work during the meetings.$qb$, $qb$Accept every meeting because invitations should take priority.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have 30 minutes before a scheduled meeting and a task needs about 45 minutes of focused work. What is the best choice?$qb$, $qb$Do a smaller useful task now and protect a full block for the 45-minute task$qb$, $qb$Prepare the first part of the task and stop when the meeting starts$qb$, $qb$Start the 45-minute task and continue after the meeting$qb$, $qb$Join the meeting late so you can finish the task$qb$, $qb$Skip the meeting without telling anyone$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A low-priority request arrives while you are working on a task due in one hour. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the request, give a realistic response time, and finish the urgent task$qb$, $qb$Tell the requester you will respond after the deadline task$qb$, $qb$Stop briefly to complete the new request$qb$, $qb$Work on both tasks together$qb$, $qb$Ignore the new request completely$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have three tasks due tomorrow, but one needs input from someone leaving early today. What should you do first?$qb$, $qb$Secure the needed input before the person leaves, then plan the remaining work$qb$, $qb$Contact the person now and explain what you need$qb$, $qb$Start the largest task first$qb$, $qb$Work in the order the tasks were assigned$qb$, $qb$Wait until tomorrow and ask someone else$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have an hour of focused work planned, but a non-urgent meeting is added at the same time. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check whether the meeting needs you; if not, protect the focus block or propose another time$qb$, $qb$Ask the organizer whether your attendance is necessary$qb$, $qb$Attend the meeting and move the focused work later$qb$, $qb$Cancel your focus block without checking$qb$, $qb$Ignore the meeting invitation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have a fixed deadline at 4 PM and a meeting invitation for 3:30 PM that does not require your decision. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Protect the deadline work and decline or propose another time if your attendance is not necessary$qb$, $qb$Ask the organizer whether you need to attend$qb$, $qb$Attend and finish the deadline work afterward$qb$, $qb$Join the meeting while continuing your task$qb$, $qb$Ignore the invitation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have two hours before a deadline and receive an optional invitation for a one-hour session. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Protect the deadline work and attend only if the session is more important or can be moved safely$qb$, $qb$Check the session purpose before deciding whether to attend$qb$, $qb$Attend because invitations should be accepted$qb$, $qb$Join the session while doing the deadline task$qb$, $qb$Ignore both the invitation and the deadline plan$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How should an associate effectively manage time when balancing high-priority analytical work with daily admin tasks?$qb$, $qb$Structuring dedicated focus blocks on calendars for priority work and batching admin tasks into fixed windows.$qb$, $qb$Completing administrative duties first each morning to clear schedules for priority analytical tasks later.$qb$, $qb$Focusing exclusively on analytical work and addressing administrative duties only when reminded by peers.$qb$, $qb$Working extended daily hours regularly to ensure both priority deliverables and admin duties are completed.$qb$, $qb$Requesting that routine administrative duties be reassigned to junior team members permanently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two critical milestones require the same specialist during the same week. One protects regulatory compliance; the other protects a major customer commitment. What is the best scheduling response?$qb$, $qb$Quantify consequences, explore scope and sequencing options, reserve the specialist where substitution is least possible, and make the residual trade-off explicit.$qb$, $qb$Give priority to the regulatory milestone because compliance always outranks commercial commitments.$qb$, $qb$Split the specialist's time evenly between both milestones.$qb$, $qb$Ask the specialist to work extended hours so neither milestone moves.$qb$, $qb$Let the two sponsors negotiate directly for the specialist's time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your calendar is dominated by recurring governance meetings while two strategic decisions keep slipping. What should you change?$qb$, $qb$Redesign the cadence around decision value: delegate or shorten low-value forums, protect preparation time, and set deadlines for strategic choices.$qb$, $qb$Block two hours every Friday and keep all meetings unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Attend every forum but ask teams to prepare the decisions for you.$qb$, $qb$Do the strategic work outside normal hours.$qb$, $qb$Postpone the decisions until the governance cycle is quieter.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your organization depends on you for too many external relationships, leaving little capacity for strategic work. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Segment relationships by where your personal involvement creates unique value, deliberately transfer others with context and authority, and protect time for enterprise decisions.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have four small tasks and one important task requiring an hour of concentration. Changing one task will affect another person's planned work. What is the best plan?$qb$, $qb$Protect an hour for the important task and group the small tasks around it$qb$, $qb$Schedule the important task first and fit small tasks into remaining time$qb$, $qb$Clear the small tasks first so they do not distract you$qb$, $qb$Work on whichever task attracts your attention$qb$, $qb$Switch tasks whenever a new request arrives$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have three important commitments this week and a stakeholder adds urgent work that cannot simply be refused. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reassess priorities and dependencies, make the trade-off visible, and agree which commitment moves before accepting the new work$qb$, $qb$Identify the least damaging task to move and tell the affected person$qb$, $qb$Accept the new work and recover time by working longer$qb$, $qb$Delay the least visible commitment quietly$qb$, $qb$Do the urgent work first and decide later what slips$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two tasks are due today, but one blocks a teammate's work. One person's delay will affect another person's planned work. Which should you do first?$qb$, $qb$Complete the blocking task first if both are equally important$qb$, $qb$Tell the teammate when you expect to finish their task$qb$, $qb$Work on the easier task first to reduce your task count$qb$, $qb$Switch between both tasks every few minutes$qb$, $qb$Choose whichever task you started first$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R2-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need uninterrupted time for a detailed task. What is the best way to protect it?$qb$, $qb$Set a focus block, mute non-urgent alerts, and tell teammates when you will next check messages.$qb$, $qb$Mute alerts until the task is complete and check them afterward.$qb$, $qb$Read each alert quickly so nothing is missed.$qb$, $qb$Keep chat open while working on the task.$qb$, $qb$Stop the task whenever a new message arrives.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are doing a careful reconciliation when an optional meeting invitation arrives. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Finish the current check, then review the invitation at a natural break$qb$, $qb$Leave the invitation unread until the reconciliation block ends$qb$, $qb$Open the invitation briefly and return to the numbers$qb$, $qb$Stop the reconciliation to decide immediately$qb$, $qb$Accept the meeting without reading the details$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are checking a contract and receive a non-urgent phone call. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Let the call wait or arrange a callback, then finish the current careful check$qb$, $qb$Send a message saying when you can call back$qb$, $qb$Answer the call and keep reading the contract$qb$, $qb$Stop the contract review immediately$qb$, $qb$Ignore all calls for the whole day$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How can an associate protect focus bandwidth when digital chat alerts interrupt deep analytical work?$qb$, $qb$Silencing non-urgent alerts, establishing scheduled async communication windows, and communicating focus blocks.$qb$, $qb$Responding to chat alerts immediately as they arrive to keep inbox counts clear while working on analysis.$qb$, $qb$Checking email folders every 15 minutes while attempting to work on analytical tasks simultaneously.$qb$, $qb$Muting communication channels for the entire workday and responding to messages after business hours.$qb$, $qb$Informing supervisors that message volumes are preventing the completion of daily analytical assignments.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your communications team receives constant requests marked 'urgent,' causing important reputation work to be fragmented. What is the best focus mechanism?$qb$, $qb$Create transparent intake criteria tied to impact and deadlines, reserve capacity for strategic work, and challenge unjustified urgency.$qb$, $qb$Handle requests in the order they arrive so prioritization is perceived as fair.$qb$, $qb$Ask each requester to obtain senior-leader approval before work begins.$qb$, $qb$Block fixed focus hours and process all urgent work outside them.$qb$, $qb$Respond fastest to the most senior requester.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your executive team receives hundreds of pages before monthly reviews, yet the same few decisions are repeatedly deferred. What should you change?$qb$, $qb$Redesign information flow around decisions: concise pre-reads, explicit asks, material exceptions and protected decision time, with detail available on demand.$qb$, $qb$Reduce every pre-read to a fixed page limit.$qb$, $qb$Ask executives to read the packs earlier.$qb$, $qb$Add a separate meeting for unresolved decisions.$qb$, $qb$Keep the packs unchanged and extend meeting duration.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A communications function is overwhelmed by requests from executives who all label their issues urgent. What operating model best protects strategic attention?$qb$, $qb$Create transparent enterprise-priority criteria, reserve capacity for true crises, delegate routine approvals, and make trade-offs visible to requesters.$qb$, $qb$Introduce a sensible safeguard but leave the underlying incentives or decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Run a small corrective action focused mainly on the most visible symptom.$qb$, $qb$Ask individual managers to compensate for the problem through extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Treat the issue as unavoidable and make no material change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A communications team faces a fast-moving public controversy with incomplete facts and pressure to respond immediately. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Establish verified facts and decision ownership, communicate what is known and unknown, correct errors quickly, and avoid filling uncertainty with speculation.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A corporate-affairs team receives conflicting requests from Legal, the CEO and regional leaders during a breaking issue. What should its head do?$qb$, $qb$Establish one decision and approval structure tied to legal obligations and stakeholder impact, surface conflicts quickly, and protect the team from parallel instruction.$qb$, $qb$In this case, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$At first, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-041$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The organization celebrates leaders who are always available, creating widespread burnout and fragile dependence on individuals. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Redesign coverage, workload and decision systems, model genuine boundaries and recovery, and make sustainable institutional capacity—not visible self-sacrifice—the leadership standard.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are halfway through a complex task when a coworker asks a non-urgent question. Frequent switching is beginning to reduce accuracy. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Finish the current logical step, then respond at a natural break$qb$, $qb$Tell them when you can respond and continue the task$qb$, $qb$Stop immediately because a coworker asked directly$qb$, $qb$Try to answer while continuing the complex task$qb$, $qb$Ignore the coworker for the rest of the day$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your work requires deep analysis, routine approvals, and frequent messages, and context switching is increasing errors. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Design protected analysis blocks, batch routine work, set response expectations, and maintain a channel only for genuine urgency$qb$, $qb$Schedule focus blocks and process routine messages at planned intervals$qb$, $qb$Keep all channels open but try not to respond immediately$qb$, $qb$Do deep work after hours$qb$, $qb$Answer messages immediately so they do not accumulate$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need 45 minutes of concentration, but non-urgent chat alerts keep appearing. Frequent switching is beginning to reduce accuracy. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Mute non-urgent alerts for the focus period and check them afterward$qb$, $qb$Tell teammates you will reply after the focus period$qb$, $qb$Read each alert quickly so nothing is missed$qb$, $qb$Keep the chat window open while working$qb$, $qb$Stop the task whenever a new message arrives$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-041$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice your accuracy dropping after a long period of focused work. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Take a short break, then return with a clear priority$qb$, $qb$Pause briefly and review the most important remaining work$qb$, $qb$Keep working at the same pace until the task is finished$qb$, $qb$Switch to several easier tasks without taking a break$qb$, $qb$Use extra caffeine and continue without stopping$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-041$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have worked late for several days and another busy week is starting. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review priorities with your manager and protect reasonable recovery time$qb$, $qb$Identify work that can be delayed or shared$qb$, $qb$Continue the same hours until the busy period ends$qb$, $qb$Reduce quality on less visible tasks$qb$, $qb$Keep the workload private so you appear dependable$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-041$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have been working at high intensity all morning and have an important review after lunch. What is the best choice?$qb$, $qb$Take a proper break and return with enough attention for the review$qb$, $qb$Step away from work briefly and prepare the review priorities afterward$qb$, $qb$Work through lunch so the review finishes earlier$qb$, $qb$Use the lunch period to answer messages$qb$, $qb$Skip the break because the review is important$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-041$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have several demanding days ahead and notice you are already sleeping less. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review workload and priorities early, protect recovery time, and raise capacity concerns if needed$qb$, $qb$Reduce nonessential work and plan proper breaks$qb$, $qb$Keep the same workload and catch up on sleep later$qb$, $qb$Use weekends to recover while maintaining long weekdays$qb$, $qb$Ignore it until performance drops$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-041$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have a long day of meetings and one task requiring careful thought. What is the best plan?$qb$, $qb$Protect a realistic focus period when your energy is likely to be strongest and include breaks$qb$, $qb$Schedule the careful task in the best available gap and reduce avoidable meetings$qb$, $qb$Do the task at the end of the day after all meetings$qb$, $qb$Work through breaks to create more time$qb$, $qb$Multitask on the task during meetings$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-041$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What practice best supports energy management during extended periods of high operational workload?$qb$, $qb$Replenishing cognitive energy through structured short rest breaks, pacing workload, and aligning priorities.$qb$, $qb$Powering through daily tasks using stimulants and extended working hours to maintain output levels.$qb$, $qb$Requesting unscheduled multi-day leave during critical project milestone execution weeks.$qb$, $qb$Reducing attention to detail on lower-visibility deliverables to save mental energy for priority tasks.$qb$, $qb$Maintaining current working routines, assuming fatigue will pass naturally once peak cycles end.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-041$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A leadership-development team has been running repeated late-night programs during a major rollout. Participation scores remain high, but facilitators are making more errors and taking sick leave. What should the program lead do?$qb$, $qb$Rebalance delivery capacity and recovery before performance degrades further, while preserving the highest-value sessions and removing avoidable load.$qb$, $qb$Keep the schedule until the rollout ends, then give facilitators additional leave.$qb$, $qb$Rotate facilitators more often without changing the overall delivery volume.$qb$, $qb$Ask facilitators to manage their personal recovery more carefully.$qb$, $qb$Replace the facilitators showing fatigue with employees who have more stamina.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A cross-departmental project is 90% complete, but the remaining 10% involves tedious final verification steps that team members are putting off. How do you drive completion?$qb$, $qb$Break down the final verification steps into discrete task assignments, establish clear daily check-ins, and actively remove lingering bottlenecks.$qb$, $qb$Remind the project team during the daily stand-up of the official project completion deadline.$qb$, $qb$Take on the remaining 10% verification tasks yourself to guarantee that the project finishes on time.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the project stall to department managers, requesting that they enforce task completion with their staff.$qb$, $qb$Mark the project as complete in tracking systems while managing remaining verification steps as post-launch maintenance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-110$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer issue is resolved technically, but the customer has not confirmed whether the solution works in their environment. The ticket is approaching an internal closure target. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Seek customer verification against the agreed success condition, make a reasonable follow-up plan, and close only under the defined policy if confirmation cannot be obtained.$qb$, $qb$Close the ticket because the technical team verified the fix.$qb$, $qb$Leave the ticket open indefinitely until the customer responds.$qb$, $qb$Ask the account manager to close the ticket.$qb$, $qb$Close it and reopen later if the customer complains.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1258$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project has closed its technical work, but several users still have temporary elevated access granted during implementation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review who still needs elevated access, remove temporary permissions that are no longer justified, confirm any continuing access has an operational owner, and then close the access action.$qb$, $qb$Leave access because users may need it later.$qb$, $qb$Remove everyone's access immediately.$qb$, $qb$Ask users to tell you if they no longer need access.$qb$, $qb$Close the project and let IT review access eventually.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1304$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A temporary project phone number is still published on customer material after the project team has disbanded. Calls now go unanswered. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Redirect or remove the number, update customer material and ownership, and verify active channels before treating the project communication setup as closed.$qb$, $qb$Ask a former project member to check the phone occasionally.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because customers should use the main number.$qb$, $qb$Disconnect the number immediately without updating material.$qb$, $qb$Wait until customers complain.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1396$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project has ended, but an automated weekly meeting invitation created for the project continues to invite twenty people. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Cancel the recurring series, preserve any needed final record, and confirm no continuing operational meeting depends on it before closing the project calendar setup.$qb$, $qb$Let people decline the meetings themselves.$qb$, $qb$Keep the series in case the team needs to reconnect.$qb$, $qb$Delete only your own calendar copy.$qb$, $qb$Wait until attendance falls to zero.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1442$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project is complete, but a temporary distribution list still sends internal updates to former project members. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove or update the obsolete distribution list after confirming any continuing operational recipients, and stop project updates from going to former members.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because extra recipients cause little harm.$qb$, $qb$Ask former members to unsubscribe themselves.$qb$, $qb$Delete the list immediately without checking ongoing use.$qb$, $qb$Keep it for six months as a precaution.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1534$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project has ended, but a temporary daily email alert created during rollout is still going to managers even though normal dashboards now show the same information. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the dashboard covers the alert's purpose, notify recipients of the normal source, and retire the temporary email alert.$qb$, $qb$Keep the alert because managers may prefer email.$qb$, $qb$Stop it immediately without checking dashboard coverage.$qb$, $qb$Reduce it to weekly emails.$qb$, $qb$Let each manager decide whether the alert continues.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-154$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You believe a customer issue is fixed, but the customer has not confirmed the result and the ticket deadline is today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to verify the result, document the solution and any remaining action, and close only when the agreed closure or no-response rule is met.$qb$, $qb$Send the solution and close the ticket, telling the customer they can reopen it if the issue continues.$qb$, $qb$Leave the ticket open until the customer replies, regardless of how long that takes.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager whether the ticket can be closed because the technical work is complete.$qb$, $qb$Close the ticket without another message because the fix has already been implemented.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-200$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project has delivered its main output, but two small defects and one documentation action remain. The team wants to mark it complete. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Agree which defects must be resolved before closure, assign the documentation action, confirm acceptance criteria, and close only when residual work is completed or formally transferred.$qb$, $qb$Mark the project complete but keep the three actions on a separate follow-up list.$qb$, $qb$Fix the two defects and leave the documentation to the operational team.$qb$, $qb$Keep the whole project open indefinitely until every minor issue disappears.$qb$, $qb$Close it now because the main output has already been delivered.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-246$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a task is finished because the file has been delivered, but the recipient has reported one unresolved question that affects how they will use it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Resolve or formally assign the question, confirm the recipient can use the deliverable as intended, and close the task only when the remaining issue has clear ownership.$qb$, $qb$Mark the task complete and answer the question separately when time allows.$qb$, $qb$Leave the task open but take no further action until the recipient follows up again.$qb$, $qb$Ask the recipient to close the task if the file itself is usable.$qb$, $qb$Close the task because delivery, not recipient interpretation, was the original commitment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2843$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new process is live, but the temporary training mailbox created for launch still receives questions that should now go to normal support. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Redirect users to normal support, close or repurpose the temporary mailbox with clear communication, and ensure outstanding questions are handed over before closure.$qb$, $qb$Keep the mailbox open indefinitely because users know it.$qb$, $qb$Close it immediately without reviewing pending messages.$qb$, $qb$Ask the training team to keep answering questions.$qb$, $qb$Ignore messages until users discover normal support themselves.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-292$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A meeting ends with five agreed actions, but two actions have no named owner. Everyone is about to leave. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm an owner and due date for each unassigned action before closing the meeting and circulate the final action record.$qb$, $qb$List the two actions as 'team actions' and ask for volunteers by email later.$qb$, $qb$Take both actions yourself so nothing is lost.$qb$, $qb$Send the minutes and ask the manager to assign the missing owners.$qb$, $qb$Leave the actions unassigned because the responsible people will recognize what belongs to them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-338$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You finish a task and hand it to another team, but they have not confirmed they understand the one remaining risk they now own. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the receiving team understands the risk, its trigger and required action, record the ownership, and close your task only after the handover is explicit.$qb$, $qb$Send another email describing the risk and close the task.$qb$, $qb$Keep the task open until the risk itself disappears.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to confirm that the receiving team now owns the risk.$qb$, $qb$Close the task because the handover email already mentioned the risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-430$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have resolved a recurring issue and documented the fix, but one temporary access permission granted during troubleshooting is still active. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove or formally review the temporary access, confirm normal controls are restored, and close the issue only after all temporary recovery measures are accounted for.$qb$, $qb$Close the issue and ask the access team to remove the permission later.$qb$, $qb$Keep the permission because it may be useful if the issue returns.$qb$, $qb$Remove the permission immediately without checking whether any recovery work still depends on it.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the access because it was not the cause of the original issue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-568$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project action is technically complete, but the evidence required for audit has not been uploaded to the agreed repository. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Upload or obtain the required evidence, verify it is linked to the action, and mark the action complete only when both work and required proof are present.$qb$, $qb$Mark it complete and upload the evidence later.$qb$, $qb$Leave it open but do not chase the evidence because the work itself is finished.$qb$, $qb$Ask audit staff to collect the evidence if they need it.$qb$, $qb$Mark it complete because audit documentation is separate from delivery.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-614$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A completed project has one temporary spreadsheet still being used because the permanent system update was delayed. The project team is about to disband. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assign ownership of the temporary tool, document its limits and retirement trigger, and transfer the permanent-system action before closing the project team.$qb$, $qb$Keep one project member assigned informally until the system update happens.$qb$, $qb$Close the project and let operations manage the spreadsheet.$qb$, $qb$Stop using the spreadsheet immediately because temporary tools should not survive project closure.$qb$, $qb$Leave the spreadsheet without an owner because everyone knows it is temporary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-706$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project team has handed over a new process, but the receiving team has not yet confirmed that the first live case worked as expected. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Agree a short post-handover validation point, confirm the first live case or defined acceptance evidence, and close the transition only when operational use is demonstrated.$qb$, $qb$Close the project because the process and documentation were handed over.$qb$, $qb$Keep the project open for several months in case problems emerge.$qb$, $qb$Ask the receiving team to contact you if the first case fails.$qb$, $qb$Run the first live case yourself even though the receiving team now owns the process.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-752$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has completed an improvement and the new process is live, but one old instruction page is still accessible and may confuse users. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove, archive or redirect the obsolete instruction, confirm users reach the current guidance, and close the change only when the old route no longer creates ambiguity.$qb$, $qb$Close the improvement and update the old page at the next documentation review.$qb$, $qb$Add a note to the old page saying a newer process exists.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team not to use the old page.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because the new process is already live.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are writing a standard operating procedure (SOP) for a new data reconciliation task that junior staff will perform. How do you structure the document?$qb$, $qb$Write concise, step-by-step instructions with visual screenshots, clearly state edge-case exception rules, and test the SOP with a junior peer.$qb$, $qb$Draft a detailed narrative explaining the background, logic, and operational goals of the new reconciliation task.$qb$, $qb$Provide bullet-point guidelines covering main steps and encourage staff to ask questions whenever they encounter edge cases.$qb$, $qb$Record a quick video walkthrough of the process and upload it to the shared drive as the primary reference guide.$qb$, $qb$Adapt an existing general department guide by adding a brief summary section specific to the reconciliation task.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-065$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process incident is resolved after several handoffs. You are asked to document what happened for future reference. What do you write?$qb$, $qb$A clear timeline of key events, decisions, owners, evidence, impact, resolution, and the preventive action agreed.$qb$, $qb$A detailed narrative containing all messages and comments in chronological order.$qb$, $qb$A short summary of the final cause and fix only.$qb$, $qb$Screenshots of the chat thread and ticket history.$qb$, $qb$A note stating that the issue was resolved and no further action is required.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-111$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team agrees to a temporary exception to a normal process for one customer. What do you document?$qb$, $qb$The exception, reason, scope, owner, approval, risks, compensating controls, start and end date, and review point.$qb$, $qb$Only the approving manager and the customer's name.$qb$, $qb$A copy of the meeting chat where the exception was discussed.$qb$, $qb$The exception and reason, without an end date.$qb$, $qb$Nothing formal because the exception is temporary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1305$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says a case must be escalated to a 'senior manager' but the organization has several different manager grades. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the required authority level or decision threshold, name it precisely in the procedure, and align teams on the same escalation route.$qb$, $qb$Use the most senior manager available.$qb$, $qb$Use the employee's direct manager.$qb$, $qb$Let each department define senior manager locally.$qb$, $qb$Keep the wording flexible so difficult cases can go higher.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1397$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A work instruction says a reviewer must have 'sufficient experience' but gives no qualification or competency standard. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the capability required for the review, define objective eligibility or authorization criteria, and update the instruction so assignments are consistent.$qb$, $qb$Use employees with at least five years of service.$qb$, $qb$Let managers decide who seems experienced enough.$qb$, $qb$Use only the most senior employee available.$qb$, $qb$Remove the experience requirement.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1581$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says an approval must be obtained 'where necessary,' but it does not say what makes approval necessary. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define the conditions, thresholds or risk triggers requiring approval and update the procedure so the decision is consistent.$qb$, $qb$Require approval for every case.$qb$, $qb$Let each employee decide when it feels necessary.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers to approve only unusual cases without defining unusual.$qb$, $qb$Remove the approval reference.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-201$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A handover document lists the right process steps but does not state who owns two critical decisions. Different users are making different choices. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the intended decision owners, update the document with exact roles and escalation conditions, and notify current users of the clarification.$qb$, $qb$Add the names of the people currently making those decisions.$qb$, $qb$Tell users verbally who should decide each issue.$qb$, $qb$Let each team decide locally because the process steps themselves are correct.$qb$, $qb$Leave the document unchanged and correct decisions only when disagreement occurs.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2363$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says 'send the report promptly,' while one team reads that as one hour and another as one business day. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Replace 'promptly' with a measurable deadline and define the relevant clock, business-day and exception rules.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate operational requirement with a localized fix while monitoring progress.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current approach and wait for other departments to adapt their workflows.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the situation to department heads without proposing potential solutions.$qb$, $qb$Disengage from the issue and let other stakeholders resolve the friction independently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-247$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure has been updated correctly, but the old version remains in a commonly used shared folder with a similar file name. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove or clearly archive the obsolete version according to document-control rules, make the current version easy to identify, and notify users who may still rely on the old copy.$qb$, $qb$Rename the old file with 'OLD' and leave both versions in the folder.$qb$, $qb$Send the current procedure to your own team and leave the shared folder unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Tell users to check the modification date before opening either file.$qb$, $qb$Do nothing because the new version is technically available in the same folder.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2844$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says records must be stored in a 'secure location' but does not identify approved systems. Teams are using different places. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm approved storage locations and access requirements, update the procedure with explicit options, and move records from unsuitable locations where needed.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team to use the folder you consider safest.$qb$, $qb$Ask each manager to choose a secure location.$qb$, $qb$Keep the wording because security needs vary.$qb$, $qb$Require all records to be stored locally on employee computers.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-293$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A contract summary correctly states the payment amount but omits a condition that changes when the payment becomes due. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the summary to include the condition, verify it against the contract, and notify anyone who may have relied on the incomplete summary.$qb$, $qb$Add the condition to your own notes and mention it when payment is discussed.$qb$, $qb$Leave the summary unchanged because the payment amount itself is correct.$qb$, $qb$Send a separate email about the condition without changing the summary.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the payment date approaches before clarifying the condition.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-339$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A meeting record says a change was 'approved,' but it does not state whether the approval was temporary or permanent. You remember it was temporary. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the decision evidence, correct the record to state the temporary scope and expiry or review point, and notify affected people of the clarification.$qb$, $qb$Add a personal note that the approval was temporary.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team verbally that the record should be interpreted as temporary.$qb$, $qb$Leave the record unchanged because everyone who attended knows what was meant.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the temporary period is about to end before correcting the record.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-477$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process document says a review is required 'regularly,' and teams are using intervals ranging from monthly to annually. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the intended frequency or risk-based rule with the process owner, replace 'regularly' with a measurable requirement, and communicate the clarified standard.$qb$, $qb$Choose a quarterly review for your team because it is a reasonable middle ground.$qb$, $qb$Add examples of monthly and annual reviews so teams can choose.$qb$, $qb$Leave the wording because different teams may need different intervals.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers to decide the frequency locally without changing the document.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-569$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says a task must be completed 'promptly,' and teams disagree whether that means hours or days. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the intended service or risk requirement, replace the vague term with a measurable timeframe or defined trigger, and communicate the clarified expectation.$qb$, $qb$Set a 24-hour target for your own team.$qb$, $qb$Tell teams to use reasonable professional judgment.$qb$, $qb$Add examples of both same-day and three-day completion.$qb$, $qb$Leave the wording because exact deadlines can reduce flexibility.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-615$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A policy summary uses the word 'may' where the underlying policy says an action 'must' be taken. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the summary to preserve the mandatory requirement, verify other material wording against the policy, and notify users who may have relied on the weaker wording.$qb$, $qb$Change 'may' to 'must' and leave the rest of the summary unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Tell users verbally that the action is actually mandatory.$qb$, $qb$Leave the summary because readers should refer to the full policy for exact wording.$qb$, $qb$Add a disclaimer that summaries are not authoritative.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-661$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process guide lists two different deadlines in separate sections because one section was updated and the other was missed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the correct deadline, update both sections and version information, search for related references, and notify users who may have relied on the inconsistent guide.$qb$, $qb$Correct the section most people use.$qb$, $qb$Add a note at the top saying the newer deadline takes priority.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team which deadline is correct without changing the guide.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the next scheduled document review to fix both sections.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-799$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A guide states that records should be retained for 'at least a few years.' Different teams are choosing different periods. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the approved retention rule, replace the vague phrase with the exact period and trigger, and align storage guidance with that requirement.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team to keep records for five years to be safe.$qb$, $qb$Keep records indefinitely.$qb$, $qb$Let each team choose a reasonable period based on risk.$qb$, $qb$Add examples of three, five and seven years.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You anticipate that upcoming holiday seasonal demand will increase customer ticket volume by 50% in six weeks. How do you prepare your team's workflow?$qb$, $qb$Forecast resource requirements, draft an updated operational schedule, build auto-response templates, and present a capacity plan to leadership.$qb$, $qb$Monitor incoming ticket volumes weekly and hire temporary contractors if daily tickets exceed standard capacity limits.$qb$, $qb$Request immediate approval for additional department overtime budget starting next month to handle the expected workload.$qb$, $qb$Advise customer service agents to streamline ticket response lengths during the holiday season to maintain resolution speed.$qb$, $qb$Wait until ticket volume increases to evaluate whether existing staff can handle the workload without structural changes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-063$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A product launch is eight weeks away. One approval normally takes two weeks, and the approver will be unavailable for one of those weeks. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Work backward from launch, secure the approval window early, prepare decision-ready material in advance, and define a backup path if timing slips.$qb$, $qb$Submit the approval request one week earlier than usual.$qb$, $qb$Continue the normal plan and ask for urgent approval if the approver is away.$qb$, $qb$Delay all other launch work until the approval is secured.$qb$, $qb$Assume another manager can approve if the normal approver is unavailable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-109$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A key project phase starts in six weeks. It depends on data from another team that has missed similar deadlines before. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Agree the required data, date, owner, and quality expectations early, set interim checkpoints, and create a contingency for partial or late data.$qb$, $qb$Send an early reminder two weeks before the data is due.$qb$, $qb$Add extra time after the expected data date.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the other team misses the deadline before escalating.$qb$, $qb$Build the final plan assuming the data will arrive on time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1257$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A yearly customer certification needs signatures from two executives. One executive's role is being replaced during the certification month. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify who will hold signing authority, secure the transition and timetable early, and ensure the new or outgoing executive can complete the requirement legitimately.$qb$, $qb$Ask the outgoing executive to sign the future certification before leaving.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the new executive starts.$qb$, $qb$Use last year's signatures.$qb$, $qb$Submit with one signature and explain later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1303$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A regulatory filing is due in five months, and the software used to generate one required report will be replaced in three months. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the required report definition, preserve or recreate the reporting capability in the new system, and test the output before the old software is retired.$qb$, $qb$Generate the filing report five months early.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the new system and assume it will contain the report.$qb$, $qb$Ask IT to keep the old software indefinitely.$qb$, $qb$Remove the report if the new system cannot produce it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1487$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring customer report depends on a specialist data extract, and that specialist will be unavailable during the next reporting cycle. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Transfer the extraction steps and access, have another person run a practice extract, and confirm definitions before the specialist becomes unavailable.$qb$, $qb$Ask the specialist to prepare the next report early.$qb$, $qb$Wait and contact them during their absence if needed.$qb$, $qb$Use the previous period's data.$qb$, $qb$Skip the specialist section for one cycle.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-153$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Last quarter, a predictable month-end surge caused backlogs and customer delays. The next surge is three weeks away and your team has the same staffing level. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the last surge to identify where capacity failed, estimate the upcoming demand, arrange preventive actions and backup support, and define indicators that will trigger extra capacity before service falls.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to prepare for a busy period and schedule additional progress checks during the final week.$qb$, $qb$Create an overtime roster now so additional hours are available if the backlog starts growing.$qb$, $qb$Wait until volumes rise before reallocating work because the forecast may be wrong.$qb$, $qb$Assume the team will handle the surge better this time because they now know what to expect.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1533$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer review next quarter needs data from an employee survey, but the survey owner is leaving before the survey closes. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Transfer survey administration, access, definitions and reporting steps to a named owner before departure, and test that the new owner can retrieve the needed data.$qb$, $qb$Ask the departing owner to close the survey early.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the survey closes and ask HR for help.$qb$, $qb$Use last year's survey data.$qb$, $qb$Ask the former employee to assist after leaving.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1579$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer contract requires an annual evidence pack, and the employee who knows where older evidence is stored plans to retire before the next pack is due. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Map the required evidence locations, transfer access and retrieval knowledge to a named owner, and run a sample retrieval before the employee leaves.$qb$, $qb$Ask the employee to prepare next year's pack early.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the annual pack is due.$qb$, $qb$Keep the employee's contact details for later questions.$qb$, $qb$Use only new evidence next year.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-291$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring service contract renews in six weeks. Last year's renewal was delayed because legal review started too late. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Work backward from the renewal date, engage legal and other dependencies now, identify required inputs and decision dates, and track early warning points for slippage.$qb$, $qb$Send the contract to legal four weeks before renewal instead of two weeks as last year.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor to extend the current contract automatically if review runs late.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the vendor's renewal proposal before involving legal.$qb$, $qb$Assume the process will be faster this year because the contract is familiar.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-337$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A major internal review is eight weeks away, and three teams must provide data in different formats. Last time, reconciliation consumed the final week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Agree common definitions and submission formats now, set staged data dates, test reconciliation early, and leave time to resolve discrepancies before the review.$qb$, $qb$Ask all teams to submit data one week earlier than last time.$qb$, $qb$Assign extra people to reconciliation during the final week.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the first submissions arrive before deciding whether formats need alignment.$qb$, $qb$Use whichever team's format is easiest and convert the others at the end.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-383$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project depends on a regulatory approval that normally takes four weeks but sometimes takes eight. The target launch is ten weeks away. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Submit as early as possible, plan around the longer credible approval window, identify work that can proceed safely in parallel, and define the latest decision point for protecting the launch.$qb$, $qb$Plan for the normal four-week approval and add one week of contingency.$qb$, $qb$Ask the regulator to guarantee the four-week timeline.$qb$, $qb$Wait until all project work is complete before submitting the approval package.$qb$, $qb$Keep the launch date unchanged and solve any approval delay if it occurs.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-429$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A critical software license expires in three months. Renewal requires budget approval, security review, and vendor paperwork from different teams. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Work backward from expiry, identify each dependency and lead time, start the longest steps early, and set decision points that preserve continuity if one approval slips.$qb$, $qb$Submit the budget request now and start the other steps after funding is approved.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor to remind you one month before expiry.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the final month because renewals are usually routine.$qb$, $qb$Assume temporary license extensions will be available if approval is late.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-475$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project needs specialist testing in six weeks, and the specialist team is already heavily booked. Your exact test date is not yet fixed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Engage the specialist team now, reserve a realistic window or agree booking triggers, and plan upstream work so the final test date can be confirmed before capacity disappears.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the exact date is known before making a booking.$qb$, $qb$Book the earliest available date even if your project may not be ready.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project sponsor to secure priority access to the specialists.$qb$, $qb$Assume the specialist team will fit the work in because the project is important.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-521$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A key external expert will be available for only one week next month, but several project decisions may need their input. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the decisions that truly require the expert, prepare the necessary evidence beforehand, reserve time during their availability, and define alternatives for issues that arise later.$qb$, $qb$Book the expert for the entire week so they are available whenever needed.$qb$, $qb$Wait until questions arise and contact the expert during their available week.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project sponsor to extend the expert's availability.$qb$, $qb$Assume internal staff can handle anything that comes up after the expert leaves.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-567$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A product launch in two months depends on packaging that has a six-week supplier lead time. The final design is still being refined. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which design elements must freeze for ordering, set the latest safe decision date, explore provisional or staged ordering, and protect a fallback if design changes continue.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the final design so the supplier receives one correct order.$qb$, $qb$Order the current design now and accept rework if it changes.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier to reduce the lead time once the design is final.$qb$, $qb$Keep the launch date and let the packaging team solve any late design impact.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-613$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A yearly audit requires evidence from several teams. Last year, some evidence could not be found because owners had left the company. The next audit is four months away. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define the required evidence and durable storage locations now, assign current owners, test retrieval before the audit, and close gaps while knowledgeable people are still available.$qb$, $qb$Ask teams to start collecting evidence one month before the audit.$qb$, $qb$Create a list of last year's missing evidence and focus only on those items.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers to keep copies of important evidence personally.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the auditor's request list because requirements may change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-751$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A specialist vendor contract ends in five months, but replacing the vendor would require three months of qualification and testing. Performance is currently acceptable. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Start renewal and alternative-market review early, define the latest decision date that preserves switching time, and gather performance evidence before the qualification window closes.$qb$, $qb$Wait until two months before expiry because current performance is acceptable.$qb$, $qb$Begin full qualification of a replacement immediately.$qb$, $qb$Assume the current vendor will renew on similar terms.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor for an automatic extension if negotiations run late.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-797$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A yearly license renewal requires a usage forecast, but the person who prepares that forecast will leave the company two months before renewal. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Capture the forecasting method and assumptions before they leave, assign a successor, run a practice forecast, and set renewal milestones so the dependency is transferred early.$qb$, $qb$Ask the person to prepare the renewal forecast before leaving even though it will be old.$qb$, $qb$Wait until renewal and ask finance to estimate usage.$qb$, $qb$Ask the departing employee to remain available after leaving.$qb$, $qb$Use last year's forecast with a growth adjustment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-843$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A critical project workshop is in seven weeks, and two decision-makers have conflicting travel schedules. Their joint decision is needed during the workshop. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Resolve availability early, identify alternate dates or decision methods, and secure a workable decision window before other workshop dependencies become fixed.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the workshop is closer because travel schedules may change.$qb$, $qb$Invite both and proceed with whoever can attend.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to delegate the decision without discussing it first.$qb$, $qb$Move the workshop online automatically.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-021$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two days before a major client workshop, your team's budget for printing printed workbooks is unexpectedly cut by 50%. How do you adapt?$qb$, $qb$Convert workbooks into interactive digital PDFs, print essential key worksheets only, and communicate the eco-friendly digital shift to attendees.$qb$, $qb$Reduce workbook length by cutting optional sections to fit within the reduced printing budget.$qb$, $qb$Request an emergency budget override from your department director to print the original workbooks as planned.$qb$, $qb$Partner with another internal team to borrow unused printing credits from their department budget.$qb$, $qb$Cancel printed materials entirely and email static PDF documents to attendees after the workshop concludes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1123$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A small site inspection requires a standard checklist, but the printed copies were left at the office. The approved checklist is available on the company intranet from a tablet. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the approved current checklist on the tablet, ensure required evidence is captured correctly, and continue the inspection without returning solely for paper copies.$qb$, $qb$Drive back for the printed copies because that is the usual method.$qb$, $qb$Use an old paper copy found at the site.$qb$, $qb$Perform the inspection from memory and complete the checklist later.$qb$, $qb$Download the checklist to a personal device.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-113$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A system outage prevents use of the normal workflow. There is an approved manual fallback, but it is slower and requires reconciliation later. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the approved fallback for priority work, record transactions accurately, communicate the temporary process, and plan reconciliation when the system returns.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the system to return so no manual work is needed.$qb$, $qb$Create a faster unofficial workaround in a personal spreadsheet.$qb$, $qb$Process all work manually without prioritizing.$qb$, $qb$Tell customers nothing can be done until the system is restored.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1169$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A site team needs to label several samples, but the label printer stops working. Approved blank labels and permanent markers are available under the contingency procedure. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the approved manual labeling method, apply the required identifiers and verification, and reconcile or reprint labels later if the procedure requires it.$qb$, $qb$Stop sampling until the printer is repaired.$qb$, $qb$Write abbreviated labels to save time.$qb$, $qb$Use ordinary sticky notes instead.$qb$, $qb$Leave samples unlabeled and identify them later from memory.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1261$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A delivery team arrives at a site and finds the normal loading bay closed. A second approved bay is open but requires a different documented route. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the alternate bay is authorized, follow the documented route and safety requirements, and continue the delivery while reporting the closure through the normal channel.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the usual bay reopens.$qb$, $qb$Use the second bay but take the familiar route.$qb$, $qb$Unload at an unofficial nearby entrance.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the delivery without checking the approved alternative.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1307$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A site team needs to scan asset tags, but the approved scanner is temporarily unavailable. The asset numbers are clearly printed and the system allows verified manual entry. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use manual entry with the required verification for the limited task, record the scanner issue, and continue rather than waiting solely for the preferred device.$qb$, $qb$Delay the asset check until the scanner returns.$qb$, $qb$Use a personal scanning application.$qb$, $qb$Enter numbers without verification to save time.$qb$, $qb$Estimate asset numbers from the previous inventory.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1399$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team needs to verify package weights, but the usual scale is being calibrated. Another approved calibrated scale is available in a nearby area. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the alternate scale is suitable for the weight range, use it under the same recording procedure, and continue the work.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the usual scale because staff know it better.$qb$, $qb$Use an uncalibrated desk scale temporarily.$qb$, $qb$Estimate weights from package contents.$qb$, $qb$Skip weight verification for this batch.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1491$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A site team needs to photograph equipment labels, but the usual company camera is being repaired. Another approved company tablet has a suitable camera. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the tablet meets the evidence and security requirements, use it under the normal file-handling process, and continue the inspection.$qb$, $qb$Delay until the normal camera returns.$qb$, $qb$Use a personal phone and email the photos later.$qb$, $qb$Write down the labels instead of photographing them.$qb$, $qb$Skip the evidence and rely on the inspection notes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
