  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-846$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have 45 minutes before a fixed deadline. A teammate asks you to proofread a non-urgent document that will be sent tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect the fixed deadline and agree a review time afterward or the next morning, unless the teammate identifies a reason the document cannot wait.$qb$, $qb$Proofread quickly because helping a teammate should take only a few minutes.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the request until tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Proofread while continuing your own work.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to find someone else even though you can help later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-938$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are due to submit a tender response in 35 minutes when a colleague asks you to join an informal discussion about next month's office event. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect the regulatory deadline and decline or provide input asynchronously unless the brainstorming genuinely requires you.$qb$, $qb$Attend because collaboration matters.$qb$, $qb$Join and work on the response simultaneously.$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to move the session.$qb$, $qb$Delay the response.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Continuous chat notifications and non-urgent email alerts frequently interrupt your deep analytical work during peak hours. How do you manage focus?$qb$, $qb$Set scheduled async communication windows, communicate focus blocks to your team, turn off non-critical alerts, and execute deep work intentionally.$qb$, $qb$Respond to chat notifications immediately as they arrive to keep your inbox clear while working on analysis.$qb$, $qb$Check emails every 15 minutes while attempting to work on analytical tasks simultaneously.$qb$, $qb$Mute chat notifications for the entire day and respond to all messages after business hours.$qb$, $qb$Inform your supervisor that communication volume is preventing you from completing daily assignments.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-086$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your role requires detailed analysis, routine approvals, and frequent team messages. You are making more mistakes because you switch tasks constantly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create protected focus blocks for high-concentration work, batch routine approvals and messages, communicate response windows, and preserve a channel for genuine urgency.$qb$, $qb$Turn off all notifications for the entire day.$qb$, $qb$Respond to messages immediately so they do not remain in your mind.$qb$, $qb$Work on analysis after normal hours when messages stop.$qb$, $qb$Ask teammates to contact your manager instead of you during analysis work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1142$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a difficult recommendation and keep checking email for a response that is useful but not required until tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a later time to check for the response, close email during a focus block, and progress the recommendation using what can be done now.$qb$, $qb$Keep email open so you see the response immediately.$qb$, $qb$Send repeated follow-ups to get the response sooner.$qb$, $qb$Stop the recommendation until the response arrives.$qb$, $qb$Switch to easier tasks for the rest of the day.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1188$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are trying to solve a complex problem but keep checking a live metric that changes every few minutes even though the next decision is not due for two hours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a meaningful next check time or threshold, close the metric view, and focus on the analysis that can change the upcoming decision.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric visible so you never miss a change.$qb$, $qb$Refresh it less often but leave it open.$qb$, $qb$Stop the analysis until the metric stabilizes.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to watch the metric continuously.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1280$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a complex forecast and keep checking a team chat where colleagues are discussing an unrelated social event. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Mute or close the chat for a defined focus period, complete the forecast work that needs concentration, and check the social discussion later.$qb$, $qb$Keep the chat open but try not to read it.$qb$, $qb$Join the discussion briefly whenever a new message appears.$qb$, $qb$Move the forecast to another day.$qb$, $qb$Ask colleagues to stop chatting until you finish.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-132$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a complex analysis while team members regularly ask quick questions that interrupt your thinking. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set predictable times for routine questions, provide an urgent escalation route, and protect focused analysis periods so accessibility and concentration are both maintained.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team not to interrupt you until the analysis is complete.$qb$, $qb$Answer every question immediately to remain supportive.$qb$, $qb$Work on the analysis at night.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to send all questions to another colleague.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1326$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a complex document and repeatedly adjust fonts and spacing while one important argument remains unresolved. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the formatting as low-value avoidance, define the unresolved argument and evidence needed, and return to presentation polish only after the reasoning is settled.$qb$, $qb$Finish formatting first so the document feels complete.$qb$, $qb$Alternate formatting and reasoning every few minutes.$qb$, $qb$Remove the unresolved argument.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to write the difficult section.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1510$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a difficult task and keep opening a completed report to make small wording improvements that are not due today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the easier polishing task as a distraction, close it, and protect a focused block for the difficult work before returning to optional improvements.$qb$, $qb$Finish polishing the report so it is fully done.$qb$, $qb$Keep both documents open and switch when stuck.$qb$, $qb$Move the difficult task to tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to polish the report while you work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1602$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a difficult presentation and keep adjusting animations that add little value while the conclusion still needs stronger evidence. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Stop the low-value formatting, identify the evidence needed for the conclusion, and return to presentation polish only after the reasoning is sound.$qb$, $qb$Finish the animations first so the deck is nearly complete.$qb$, $qb$Alternate between animations and evidence gathering.$qb$, $qb$Remove the conclusion.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to find the evidence while you format.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-222$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are halfway through a detailed task when you notice you have opened email, chat, and several unrelated tabs without intending to. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Close the unrelated windows, define a focused work interval, note anything you genuinely need to revisit, and schedule a specific time to check messages afterward.$qb$, $qb$Close email and chat but leave the unrelated tabs open in case you need them.$qb$, $qb$Finish answering current messages so you can return with a clear inbox.$qb$, $qb$Move the detailed work to later in the day when you expect fewer interruptions.$qb$, $qb$Continue switching because checking each item takes only a few seconds.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2834$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a difficult analysis and notice yourself repeatedly replying to non-urgent messages because each reply feels easy to finish. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a communication check time, close non-urgent messaging during a focused block, and return to the analysis until the planned break.$qb$, $qb$Keep messaging open but try to reply less.$qb$, $qb$Finish all easy messages first to clear your mind.$qb$, $qb$Move the analysis to tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to handle the analysis.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-314$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a complex document when a colleague starts an interesting but non-urgent conversation beside you. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague you want to continue the conversation later, note a suitable time if needed, and return your attention to the complex document.$qb$, $qb$Continue talking for a few minutes and then return to work.$qb$, $qb$Keep working while listening and responding to the colleague.$qb$, $qb$Move to another location without explaining why.$qb$, $qb$Stop the document because maintaining good colleague relationships is more important than a short period of focus.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-360$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are writing a detailed proposal when you notice that every new email notification makes you read the subject line even if you do not reply. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Turn off visible notifications for a defined focus period, set a planned email-check time, and preserve an urgent-contact route if your role requires one.$qb$, $qb$Keep notifications on but train yourself not to read the subject lines.$qb$, $qb$Check each email quickly so it stops distracting you.$qb$, $qb$Move the proposal work to the evening when fewer emails arrive.$qb$, $qb$Accept the distraction because reading a subject line takes very little time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-452$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a difficult task when you notice yourself repeatedly checking progress metrics that change only once a day. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove the metric from immediate view, set a sensible time to check it, and return attention to the task whose progress you can actually influence now.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric open but try to check it less often.$qb$, $qb$Check it one final time and then close it.$qb$, $qb$Pause the difficult task until the metric updates so you know whether your work is still needed.$qb$, $qb$Continue checking because staying informed helps motivation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-498$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a complex calculation when colleagues nearby begin discussing a topic that interests you but does not involve your work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the pull on your attention, reduce the distraction by moving or using an appropriate focus method, and return to the calculation without joining the discussion.$qb$, $qb$Listen while continuing the calculation because you do not need to participate.$qb$, $qb$Pause the calculation briefly to hear the interesting part.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleagues to stop talking because you need concentration.$qb$, $qb$Continue working in the same environment and accept that some distraction is unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-636$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a difficult analysis when you notice you are repeatedly reorganizing your notes instead of making the key judgment. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the low-value organizing as avoidance, define the decision question, work on the evidence needed for that judgment, and postpone cosmetic organization until afterward.$qb$, $qb$Finish organizing the notes so the analysis becomes easier.$qb$, $qb$Switch to another task until you feel ready to make the judgment.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to make the key judgment from your notes.$qb$, $qb$Continue organizing because good structure is essential to analysis.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-774$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a demanding task when you notice yourself checking a shared chat for reactions to a message you posted earlier. No response is needed today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a later check time, close the chat during the focus block, and return to the demanding task rather than repeatedly seeking non-urgent social feedback.$qb$, $qb$Keep the chat open but stop refreshing it manually.$qb$, $qb$Send another message to encourage replies sooner.$qb$, $qb$Pause the demanding task until someone responds.$qb$, $qb$Continue checking because knowing colleagues' reactions may affect your work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-820$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are analyzing a difficult issue when you notice you keep opening the task list to check easier work you could finish quickly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the pull toward easy completion, set a bounded period for the difficult analysis, hide the task list temporarily, and return to easier work afterward.$qb$, $qb$Complete a few easy tasks first to build momentum.$qb$, $qb$Keep the task list open but promise yourself not to switch.$qb$, $qb$Move the difficult issue to tomorrow when you have more energy.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to analyze the difficult issue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-866$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are drafting an important recommendation and keep switching to formatting the slides because it feels easier than resolving one uncertain conclusion. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Park the formatting, define what evidence or judgment the uncertain conclusion needs, and spend a focused block resolving that issue before returning to presentation work.$qb$, $qb$Finish formatting first so the deck is ready.$qb$, $qb$Alternate between formatting and analysis to avoid getting stuck.$qb$, $qb$Remove the uncertain conclusion from the presentation.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to decide the conclusion.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-041$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a month of heavy project activity, you notice signs of mental fatigue impacting your daily focus and accuracy. How do you manage your well-being?$qb$, $qb$Incorporate short structured rest breaks into your day, prioritize high-energy tasks during peak focus hours, and discuss workload pacing with your lead.$qb$, $qb$Power through daily tasks using coffee and extended hours to maintain productivity levels until the project finishes.$qb$, $qb$Request three days of immediate unscheduled leave during a critical project milestone week.$qb$, $qb$Reduce attention to detail on lower-visibility tasks to save mental energy for priority items.$qb$, $qb$Maintain current working routines, assuming fatigue will pass naturally once project pressure eases.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-087$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team has worked late for three weeks to meet a surge in demand. Output remains high, but errors and fatigue are increasing. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Raise the sustainability risk, review priorities and capacity, reduce or redistribute lower-value work, and protect recovery rather than treating overtime as the operating plan.$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to take more short breaks while keeping the same workload.$qb$, $qb$Continue overtime until demand returns to normal.$qb$, $qb$Offer a team lunch to improve morale.$qb$, $qb$Lower quality expectations temporarily without discussing the workload.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1143$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has been using unused leave days to cope with workload peaks, and several employees now have large leave balances near year-end. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the leave accumulation as a capacity-planning signal, arrange coverage and reprioritization so leave can be taken, and address the workload pattern causing repeated deferral.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to carry the leave forward if policy allows.$qb$, $qb$Tell everyone to book leave immediately without changing workload.$qb$, $qb$Pay out leave and keep the current workload.$qb$, $qb$Wait until year-end and deal with balances individually.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1281$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has been meeting targets by leaving several vacancies unfilled and distributing the work among existing staff. Quality complaints are beginning to increase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Connect the complaint trend to capacity and workload evidence, reassess vacancy and priority decisions, and restore sustainable capacity before quality deteriorates further.$qb$, $qb$Keep the vacancies open because targets are still being met.$qb$, $qb$Tell staff to focus more carefully on quality.$qb$, $qb$Add extra quality checks without changing workload.$qb$, $qb$Wait until targets are missed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1327$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has met targets for months by asking the same few high performers to take extra work whenever demand spikes. Those employees are now taking more unplanned leave. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the concentration of extra work and leave pattern as a sustainability risk, rebalance skills and workload, and build broader capacity rather than relying on the same people.$qb$, $qb$Keep using the high performers because they deliver reliably.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to take planned leave only during quiet periods.$qb$, $qb$Pay them more for extra work.$qb$, $qb$Wait until their performance drops.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-133$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You personally have been covering a vacant role for a month while maintaining your normal responsibilities. Performance is still good, but recovery time is shrinking. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Raise the capacity issue early, clarify what can be deprioritized or shared, and establish a sustainable interim plan rather than relying on indefinite extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Continue until your performance actually declines.$qb$, $qb$Take a day off without changing the workload plan.$qb$, $qb$Work faster on routine tasks to create more time.$qb$, $qb$Avoid telling your manager because the vacancy is temporary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1511$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team repeatedly uses its most experienced employee to cover absences, and that employee has started postponing their own development and leave. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the repeated dependency as a resilience risk, cross-train others, rebalance cover, and protect the experienced employee's development and recovery time.$qb$, $qb$Keep using them because they are the most reliable cover.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to take development outside busy periods.$qb$, $qb$Pay them extra for covering absences.$qb$, $qb$Wait until they refuse additional cover.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1603$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team relies on one employee to answer questions about an old system, and that employee is regularly interrupted throughout the day. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Capture the recurring knowledge, train additional people, create a usable reference, and protect the employee from avoidable interruption while retaining escalation for unusual cases.$qb$, $qb$Keep routing questions to them because they answer fastest.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to work longer hours to finish their own tasks.$qb$, $qb$Move all old-system work permanently to them.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the system is retired.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-177$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have worked late for several days, your concentration is falling, and another busy week is starting. The workload will not disappear by itself. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review priorities and workload, protect recovery time, and raise capacity or trade-off decisions early if the demand cannot be handled sustainably.$qb$, $qb$Take one quieter evening and see whether your concentration improves before changing the workload plan.$qb$, $qb$Push through the next week and schedule a longer break once the peak is over.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the quality standard on less visible tasks so you can conserve energy.$qb$, $qb$Keep the problem private and rely on extra caffeine and shorter breaks until the workload falls.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-223$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has met several deadlines by repeatedly working late. The next project plan assumes the same pattern will continue. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Challenge the assumption that repeated late work is normal capacity, review scope and staffing, and build realistic workload and recovery limits into the new plan.$qb$, $qb$Accept some late work during peak periods but add a recovery day after each major deadline.$qb$, $qb$Keep the plan and ask people to volunteer for overtime rather than assigning it.$qb$, $qb$Reduce quality checks during busy weeks so people can finish earlier.$qb$, $qb$Use the same pattern because the team has proved it can meet deadlines this way.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2849$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has been covering a vacancy by working through breaks and staying late. Output remains high, but sickness absence is starting to rise. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the absence and workload pattern as a sustainability signal, reassess capacity and priorities, and arrange support rather than relying on continued extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Keep the arrangement because output is high.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees to take better care of themselves outside work.$qb$, $qb$Offer overtime pay and continue.$qb$, $qb$Wait until output falls before changing the plan.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-315$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that you are making more small errors after several weeks of consistently long days, although you are still meeting deadlines. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the error pattern as a capacity signal, review workload and priorities, restore meaningful recovery, and discuss trade-offs early if the current demand cannot be sustained.$qb$, $qb$Take one evening off and continue the same workload afterward.$qb$, $qb$Work more slowly and check everything twice while keeping the same hours.$qb$, $qb$Accept the errors as the normal cost of a busy period.$qb$, $qb$Hide the errors and correct them quietly so others do not question your capacity.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-361$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project schedule leaves no recovery time after three consecutive high-intensity milestones. The team says they can manage if necessary. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review whether all milestone dates and scope are genuinely fixed, build realistic recovery or capacity relief into the plan, and escalate trade-offs before sustained overload becomes the operating assumption.$qb$, $qb$Keep the schedule but encourage everyone to take short breaks between milestones.$qb$, $qb$Ask for volunteers who are willing to work the extra hours.$qb$, $qb$Reduce documentation and review work during the intense period.$qb$, $qb$Accept the plan because the team has said they can manage.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-407$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are planning a project after a previous project succeeded only because several people worked most weekends. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the weekend work as evidence that the previous capacity plan was insufficient, estimate the new work realistically, and adjust scope, staffing or dates instead of budgeting unpaid recovery time again.$qb$, $qb$Keep the same plan but warn the team earlier that some weekend work may be needed.$qb$, $qb$Ask who is willing to work weekends before finalizing the schedule.$qb$, $qb$Reduce quality checks so weekend work is less likely.$qb$, $qb$Use the previous plan because it ultimately delivered successfully.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-453$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member volunteers for extra work after several busy weeks, but you know they have already been working unusually long hours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss their current workload and recovery honestly, assess whether the extra work is sustainable, and find another capacity solution if accepting the offer would extend an unhealthy pattern.$qb$, $qb$Accept the help but ask them to take time off after the deadline.$qb$, $qb$Decline the offer immediately because they have already worked enough.$qb$, $qb$Accept because they volunteered and therefore understand their own capacity.$qb$, $qb$Give them the work but reduce quality expectations to make it manageable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-499$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project team has started skipping breaks to recover a schedule delay. Output is improving, but small errors are also increasing. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the error increase as a capacity and fatigue signal, reassess the recovery plan, protect reasonable breaks and critical checks, and adjust scope, support or timing if needed.$qb$, $qb$Keep the recovery effort but remind everyone to check their work more carefully.$qb$, $qb$Require a short break once per day while keeping the rest of the plan unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Accept some small errors until the schedule is recovered.$qb$, $qb$Ask people who feel tired to opt out while others continue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-545$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague has taken on extra work for several weeks and now says they cannot take planned leave because the team depends on them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the dependency and workload, arrange coverage or reprioritization so leave can be taken where possible, and address why the team has become dependent on one person's continued availability.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to postpone leave until the busy period ends.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to take leave and let the team manage without preparation.$qb$, $qb$Offer to pay or reward them for cancelling leave.$qb$, $qb$Accept their choice because they volunteered to stay available.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-775$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has covered a vacancy for two months by distributing the work among existing employees. Targets are still being met, but overtime is steadily increasing. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the overtime trend as evidence that the temporary arrangement is not cost-free, reassess hiring or temporary support, and adjust workload before sustained overcapacity becomes normal.$qb$, $qb$Keep the arrangement because targets are still being met.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to reduce overtime while keeping the same workload.$qb$, $qb$Offer overtime payments and continue until the vacancy is filled.$qb$, $qb$Wait until targets begin to slip before changing the plan.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-867$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has maintained output by repeatedly cancelling internal learning time whenever workload rises. Skill gaps are now starting to slow work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the skill gaps as a consequence of chronic capacity choices, protect targeted learning time, and adjust workload or priorities so capability development is not always the first activity removed.$qb$, $qb$Cancel more learning until workload stabilizes.$qb$, $qb$Add training outside normal hours.$qb$, $qb$Keep output targets and ask employees to learn independently.$qb$, $qb$Wait until performance drops further before restoring learning time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-FIB-001$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Telling someone early that you will be late gives them time to ______.$qb$, $qb$adjust$qb$, $qb$pay$qb$, $qb$wait$qb$, $qb$complain$qb$, $qb$understand$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
