  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-FIB-016$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Finishing a task at eighty per cent quality on time can be worth more than perfect work delivered ______.$qb$, $qb$late$qb$, $qb$jointly$qb$, $qb$quietly$qb$, $qb$publicly$qb$, $qb$informally$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-FIB-017$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Protecting one hour with no interruptions often achieves more than a whole day of ______ work.$qb$, $qb$broken$qb$, $qb$steady$qb$, $qb$scientific$qb$, $qb$quiet$qb$, $qb$planned$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-018$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are running fifteen minutes late for a site visit because of traffic. What is the best action?$qb$, $qb$Call as soon as you know, with a realistic time, so they can decide what to do$qb$, $qb$Call when you reach a safe place to stop and tell them you are delayed$qb$, $qb$Send a message saying you are delayed and that you are on your way$qb$, $qb$Drive faster where you can, since a short delay may not be noticed$qb$, $qb$Arrive and apologise then, since calling while driving is not safe or useful$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-019$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have three tasks due today and cannot finish all of them. What should you do first?$qb$, $qb$Find out which one others are waiting on, and tell the rest what to expect$qb$, $qb$Ask which of the three matters most, then work through them in that order$qb$, $qb$Start with the one that will take longest, so it is not left until the end$qb$, $qb$Start with the two shortest, so at least most of the tasks get finished$qb$, $qb$Work through them in the order they arrived and see how far you get$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague regularly arrives ten minutes into your team's morning meeting. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Speak to them about it, since the team is repeating information for one person$qb$, $qb$Speak to them and ask whether something makes the start time difficult$qb$, $qb$Start on time regardless, so the meeting is not shaped around one person$qb$, $qb$Move the meeting later, so that everyone can arrive comfortably on time$qb$, $qb$Let it go, since ten minutes is short and they catch up quickly enough$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-021$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have been given a task with no deadline attached. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Agree a date now, so it does not sit behind everything that does have one$qb$, $qb$Ask when it is needed by, so it can be placed against your other work$qb$, $qb$Set your own date and let the person know when you plan to finish it$qb$, $qb$Fit it in around your other work whenever you have spare capacity$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone asks about it, since it clearly is not urgent yet$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-022$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$It is one o'clock. A job you expected to take two hours has already taken one, and you now see it needs three more. You also promised someone a separate two-hour piece of work by five. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell whoever is waiting on the five o'clock item now, while they still have options$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager the estimate has changed and ask which should take priority$qb$, $qb$Work faster on the current job and start the other one as soon as you finish$qb$, $qb$Finish the current job properly and explain the delay on the other afterwards$qb$, $qb$Carry on and see how far you get, since the estimate may still be wrong$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-023$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your day is repeatedly broken up by short interruptions. What is most likely to help?$qb$, $qb$Set aside a fixed period each day when you are not available, and tell people$qb$, $qb$Batch the interruptions by dealing with them at two set points in the day$qb$, $qb$Ask colleagues to save non-urgent questions rather than bringing them straight away$qb$, $qb$Work faster between interruptions so the lost time is made up during the day$qb$, $qb$Accept them as part of the job and stay later to finish the main work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-024$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A meeting you are chairing has ten minutes left and two agenda items remain. What is best?$qb$, $qb$Say what will not be covered, and agree now when those items will be dealt with$qb$, $qb$Take the more important item now and move the other to the next meeting$qb$, $qb$Ask the group which of the two they would rather cover in the time left$qb$, $qb$Run over by ten minutes so both items get the discussion they need$qb$, $qb$Cover both quickly, so nothing has to be carried over to another meeting$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-025$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You promised a report by Thursday and now know it will be Monday. It is Tuesday. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them today, so they have three days to plan around the new date$qb$, $qb$Tell them today and explain what has caused the work to take longer$qb$, $qb$Tell them on Wednesday, once you are certain the date has really moved$qb$, $qb$Tell them on Thursday when the report is due, with the revised date$qb$, $qb$Work through the weekend and try to deliver something close to Thursday$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-026$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are given a large piece of work due in six weeks. What is the best way to start?$qb$, $qb$Work out the steps and set dates for each, so slippage shows up early$qb$, $qb$Break it into stages and start with the part that is least well understood$qb$, $qb$Book regular time in the diary each week to work on it steadily$qb$, $qb$Start straight away on the first part and keep going until it is done$qb$, $qb$Wait until you have a clearer picture, since six weeks is plenty of time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-027$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two people ask you for the same slot in your diary. Both say it is urgent. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask each what happens if theirs waits a day, then decide and tell both$qb$, $qb$Ask each what the deadline actually is and give the slot to the tighter one$qb$, $qb$Give the slot to whoever asked first and offer the other a later time$qb$, $qb$Split the slot between them so that both get some time with you$qb$, $qb$Give the slot to the more senior person and rearrange the other later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-028$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You consistently underestimate how long your tasks take. What is most likely to help?$qb$, $qb$Record actual times for a few weeks, then estimate from that rather than from memory$qb$, $qb$Add a fixed margin to every estimate you give, to allow for the pattern$qb$, $qb$Break tasks into smaller parts, since small pieces are easier to estimate$qb$, $qb$Take on fewer tasks, so there is more room when they overrun$qb$, $qb$Work faster, since the estimates are reasonable and the pace is the problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-029$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A weekly task takes you two hours and produces something nobody has asked about in months. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask the recipients whether it is still needed before spending more time on it$qb$, $qb$Stop it for a month and see whether anyone raises its absence$qb$, $qb$Reduce it to a shorter version that takes less of your time each week$qb$, $qb$Keep doing it, since it may be used by someone you are not aware of$qb$, $qb$Keep doing it, since it has been part of the role for a long time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-030$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are asked to attend a meeting where you are not sure you are needed. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what is needed from you, so you can attend only the part that requires you$qb$, $qb$Ask whether you are needed for the whole meeting or just one item$qb$, $qb$Ask for the notes afterwards instead, so your time is not spent on it$qb$, $qb$Attend the whole meeting, since it is easier than questioning the invitation$qb$, $qb$Decline it, since your time is better spent on the work you already have$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-031$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have twenty minutes between two appointments. What is the best use of it?$qb$, $qb$A task you had already identified as fitting a short gap like this$qb$, $qb$A short task from your list that can be finished within the time$qb$, $qb$Clearing messages, since twenty minutes is not enough for real work$qb$, $qb$Preparing for the next appointment, since it is about to start$qb$, $qb$Taking a break, since short gaps are too broken up to be useful$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-032$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer asks you to do something today that would push your other work into tomorrow. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Check who is affected by the delay before agreeing, then tell them either way$qb$, $qb$Explain what it would displace and ask the customer how urgent theirs is$qb$, $qb$Agree, then tell the affected people that their work has moved to tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Agree, since customer requests should come ahead of internal work$qb$, $qb$Decline, since your existing commitments were made before this request$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-033$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team's handover often overruns and the next shift starts late. What is the best first step?$qb$, $qb$Time what actually happens in the handover, so the overrun can be shown$qb$, $qb$Set a finish time for the handover and stop at that point each day$qb$, $qb$Ask the team what part of the handover takes longest to get through$qb$, $qb$Start the handover earlier, so it finishes before the next shift begins$qb$, $qb$Accept it, since a thorough handover matters more than the start time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-MCQ-034$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realise you have double-booked yourself for tomorrow morning. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell both today and offer each a new time, rather than asking them to sort it$qb$, $qb$Tell both today and ask which of them is able to move their meeting$qb$, $qb$Tell the one that seems less important and ask them to rearrange$qb$, $qb$Attend the more important one and apologise to the other afterwards$qb$, $qb$Attend part of each, so neither party is left entirely without you$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-035$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You drive a delivery round. A customer near the start of the route always keeps you talking for ten minutes, which makes the rest of the round run late. You do not want to be rude. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Tell them at the start that you have five minutes, then keep to it each visit$qb$, $qb$Tell them your round is running tight and that you cannot stay long today$qb$, $qb$Move them to the end of the round, so the overrun affects nobody else$qb$, $qb$Cut the conversation short when you notice the time getting away from you$qb$, $qb$Let it continue, since the customer relationship matters more than a few minutes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-036$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You work in a salon. Your first appointment ran over by twenty minutes and everyone after is now waiting. Three more clients are booked this morning. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Tell each waiting client now how late you are running and what their options are$qb$, $qb$Tell the waiting clients you are running late and apologise for the wait$qb$, $qb$Work faster through the next appointments to bring the schedule back on time$qb$, $qb$Say nothing and try to make up the time, since clients dislike hearing about delays$qb$, $qb$Carry on as normal, since clients understand that salons run late sometimes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-037$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are studying part-time alongside work. Every week you plan to study on Sunday and every week something else fills the day. How do you fix this?$qb$, $qb$Move study to a fixed time that is harder to displace, and treat it as booked$qb$, $qb$Block Sunday morning in advance and tell the people who usually ask for it$qb$, $qb$Work out what keeps filling the day, so the pattern can be broken$qb$, $qb$Try harder to protect Sunday, since the plan itself is a reasonable one$qb$, $qb$Study whenever a gap appears during the week, since Sundays are unreliable$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-038$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a site supervisor. A delivery due at nine has not arrived by ten, and six workers are standing idle. The supplier is not answering. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Move the team onto work that does not need the delivery, then keep chasing$qb$, $qb$Find other work for the team while you continue trying to reach the supplier$qb$, $qb$Keep calling the supplier, since the team cannot start without the materials$qb$, $qb$Send the team on an early break so the waiting time is not entirely wasted$qb$, $qb$Wait for the delivery, since moving the team would disrupt the day's plan$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-039$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You manage a small office. A colleague asks for 'five minutes' several times a day, and each one turns into twenty. Your own work is slipping. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Agree a set time each day for their questions, and hold the rest of your day$qb$, $qb$Tell them you have five minutes now and stop the conversation at that point$qb$, $qb$Ask them to send questions in writing so you can answer them together$qb$, $qb$Answer quickly each time, so the interruptions take up as little time as possible$qb$, $qb$Keep helping as you have been, since being available is part of the role$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-040$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have a report due tomorrow. A senior manager asks you to take on something urgent today. You cannot do both. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Tell them what the report is for and its deadline, and ask which should give way$qb$, $qb$Explain that the report is due tomorrow and ask whether theirs can wait a day$qb$, $qb$Take on the urgent task and tell the report's recipient it will now be late$qb$, $qb$Take on the urgent task and try to finish the report late this evening$qb$, $qb$Take on the urgent task, since a request from a senior manager comes first$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-041$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You work in a warehouse where the closing stock count regularly runs past finishing time. The team stays late without being asked. How do you address this?$qb$, $qb$Start the count earlier, and show what time it actually needs to be done properly$qb$, $qb$Raise with the manager that the count does not fit the time allowed for it$qb$, $qb$Ask the team where the count slows down, so the process can be shortened$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to work faster so the count finishes within the shift$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since the team is willing and the count does get completed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-042$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have agreed to help a colleague this afternoon. This morning your own workload has grown and you can no longer spare the time. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Tell them now, so they can find someone else while the afternoon is still free$qb$, $qb$Tell them now and explain that your own work has grown unexpectedly today$qb$, $qb$Give them a shorter amount of time than promised, so they get something$qb$, $qb$Help them as promised and stay late to catch up on your own work$qb$, $qb$Tell them at lunchtime once you are sure you really cannot make the time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-043$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are covering a reception desk. Walk-in visitors keep arriving while you are on the phone, and both groups end up waiting. How do you manage this?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge each visitor as they arrive and give them a realistic wait time$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge visitors with a gesture so they know they have been seen$qb$, $qb$Finish each call properly before turning to whoever is waiting at the desk$qb$, $qb$Deal with visitors first, since they are physically present and waiting$qb$, $qb$Continue as you are, since both groups will be dealt with eventually$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-044$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team's weekly meeting is scheduled for an hour and always uses the full hour, though the business usually takes about thirty minutes. How do you address this?$qb$, $qb$Cut the booking to thirty minutes, since the work will fit the time given$qb$, $qb$Set an agenda with times against each item, and keep the meeting to them$qb$, $qb$Ask the team whether the full hour is needed for the topics covered$qb$, $qb$Finish early when the business is done, rather than filling the hour$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since the extra time gives the team space for wider discussion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-045$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a nurse on a ward. Your medication round is due but a patient's family has arrived with questions that will take time. How do you handle this?$qb$, $qb$Tell the family when you can talk properly, and start the round on time$qb$, $qb$Tell the family you will come back to them after the medication round$qb$, $qb$Answer their most urgent question now and arrange to talk fully later$qb$, $qb$Talk to the family now, since they have travelled and are waiting$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to take your round so you can speak with the family$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-046$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing for an exam in three weeks and have not started. You keep planning to begin 'properly' at the weekend. How do you break the pattern?$qb$, $qb$Start today with one small piece, and book the same slot for tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Set out what needs covering and put dates against each part of it$qb$, $qb$Begin this weekend as planned, but with a clear list of what to cover$qb$, $qb$Wait until the weekend when you can give it your full attention$qb$, $qb$Study intensively in the final week, when the pressure will help you focus$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-047$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client emails asking for an update. You have nothing new to report and are busy. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Reply briefly today saying there is nothing new and when you will next update them$qb$, $qb$Reply today to say you have received it and will come back with detail$qb$, $qb$Reply once you have something to report, so the update is worth reading$qb$, $qb$Reply at the end of the week when the picture is likely to be clearer$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since replying with nothing new does not help the client$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-048$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You manage a team of four. One member finishes everything early and then waits; another is always behind. Neither has raised it. How do you handle this?$qb$, $qb$Rebalance the work between them, and set check-in points so slippage shows early$qb$, $qb$Talk to each about their workload and adjust what they are each carrying$qb$, $qb$Ask the one who is behind what is taking longer than expected$qb$, $qb$Move some work from the one who is behind to the one who finishes early$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since both are managing and neither has asked for a change$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-049$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a teacher. Marking regularly spills into your evenings and weekends, and it is getting worse each term. How do you address it?$qb$, $qb$Time how long marking actually takes, and set that against the time available$qb$, $qb$Set fixed marking sessions each week and keep the work inside them$qb$, $qb$Ask colleagues how they manage the same volume of marking each term$qb$, $qb$Mark faster, since the volume is fixed and the time has to come from somewhere$qb$, $qb$Accept it, since marking outside hours is a normal part of teaching$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-TMP-SJT-050$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have been late to work three times this month because of an unreliable bus. Nobody has said anything yet. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Change to an earlier bus now, and tell your manager what you have done$qb$, $qb$Leave earlier from now on, so the bus delay no longer makes you late$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager about the bus problem before it is raised with you$qb$, $qb$Explain the bus problem if your lateness is mentioned by anyone$qb$, $qb$Carry on as you are, since the delay is the bus company's fault$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
