  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-FIB-002$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Writing 'by Friday' is clearer than 'soon' because it can be ______.$qb$, $qb$checked$qb$, $qb$signed$qb$, $qb$agreed$qb$, $qb$shared$qb$, $qb$forgotten$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-FIB-003$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A record written on the day is more reliable than one written from ______ a week later.$qb$, $qb$memory$qb$, $qb$notes$qb$, $qb$habit$qb$, $qb$reports$qb$, $qb$guesswork$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-FIB-004$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Naming the person responsible in a written action stops it becoming ______.$qb$, $qb$everyone's$qb$, $qb$urgent$qb$, $qb$priced$qb$, $qb$formal$qb$, $qb$public$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-FIB-008$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Recording the reason for a decision helps the next person avoid repeating the same ______.$qb$, $qb$debate$qb$, $qb$meeting$qb$, $qb$schedule$qb$, $qb$handover$qb$, $qb$template$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-FIB-009$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A measurement written without its unit is not a fact but a ______.$qb$, $qb$number$qb$, $qb$signature$qb$, $qb$note$qb$, $qb$record$qb$, $qb$label$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-FIB-010$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Using the same word for the same thing throughout a document removes the reader's need to ______.$qb$, $qb$interpret$qb$, $qb$sign$qb$, $qb$translate$qb$, $qb$remember$qb$, $qb$concentrate$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-FIB-011$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A document with no date leaves the reader unable to tell whether it is still ______.$qb$, $qb$current$qb$, $qb$laminated$qb$, $qb$written$qb$, $qb$official$qb$, $qb$complete$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-FIB-012$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Copying figures by hand between two systems adds a step where an error can ______.$qb$, $qb$enter$qb$, $qb$hide$qb$, $qb$grow$qb$, $qb$survive$qb$, $qb$repeat$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-FIB-013$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Writing 'approximately 50' is more useful than writing 'approximately', because the reader needs a ______.$qb$, $qb$number$qb$, $qb$stamp$qb$, $qb$file$qb$, $qb$date$qb$, $qb$source$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-FIB-015$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A document is clearer when it states its limits as well as its ______.$qb$, $qb$contents$qb$, $qb$binding$qb$, $qb$author$qb$, $qb$length$qb$, $qb$format$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-FIB-016$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$A correction made without a note of what changed leaves no way to trace the ______.$qb$, $qb$error$qb$, $qb$layout$qb$, $qb$filename$qb$, $qb$author$qb$, $qb$folder$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-FIB-017$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$If two people must both act on a written instruction, the instruction should say who acts ______.$qb$, $qb$first$qb$, $qb$publicly$qb$, $qb$formally$qb$, $qb$quietly$qb$, $qb$separately$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-018$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You take a phone message for a colleague. The caller gives a name, a number and a reason for calling. What makes the message most useful to your colleague?$qb$, $qb$All three details plus the time of the call and who took it$qb$, $qb$All three details written down exactly as the caller gave them$qb$, $qb$The name and number, with a short summary of the reason$qb$, $qb$The name and number, since your colleague can ask the rest$qb$, $qb$A note that someone called, since the caller will probably try again$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A written procedure says 'check the equipment regularly'. What is the main problem with this?$qb$, $qb$It gives no interval, so two people could reasonably check at different times$qb$, $qb$The word 'regularly' will mean different things to different readers$qb$, $qb$It does not say what the check should actually involve$qb$, $qb$It assumes the reader already knows the right routine$qb$, $qb$It is too short to work as a written procedure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-020$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are writing minutes for a meeting where a decision was made after long discussion. What should the minutes contain?$qb$, $qb$The decision, who is doing what by when, and the reason it was chosen$qb$, $qb$The decision, the actions agreed, and who is responsible for each$qb$, $qb$The decision and a summary of the main points of the discussion$qb$, $qb$A full account of the discussion so the reasoning is preserved$qb$, $qb$The decision only, since the discussion is no longer relevant$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-021$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You find two versions of a document on the shared drive with different figures in them. Neither is marked as current. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Find out which is current, then remove or clearly mark the other one$qb$, $qb$Find out which is current and use that one for your own work$qb$, $qb$Ask the author which version people should be working from$qb$, $qb$Use the one with the later file date, since it is probably newer$qb$, $qb$Use either, since the difference in figures is likely to be minor$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-022$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague sends you a spreadsheet where one column has no heading. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what the column contains and add the heading before it goes further$qb$, $qb$Ask what the column contains so you can use the spreadsheet correctly$qb$, $qb$Work out what the column contains from the values in it$qb$, $qb$Ignore that column, since you can complete your work without it$qb$, $qb$Use it as it is, since the person who sent it clearly understands it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-023$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are recording a customer complaint. The customer is upset and describes several things at once. What should your written record contain?$qb$, $qb$Each separate issue listed on its own, with the customer's own words kept$qb$, $qb$Each separate issue listed, written in clear neutral language$qb$, $qb$A summary of the complaint that covers the main points raised$qb$, $qb$A description of the complaint and how upset the customer was$qb$, $qb$The single issue that seems most important to the customer$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-024$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A safety log has a box for 'action taken'. Someone has written 'sorted'. What is wrong with this?$qb$, $qb$Nobody reading it later can tell what was done or whether it worked$qb$, $qb$It does not describe what action was actually taken at the time$qb$, $qb$It is too brief to be a useful entry in a safety log$qb$, $qb$It is informal language for an official safety record$qb$, $qb$It does not say who carried out the action that was taken$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-025$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are asked to write instructions for a task you do every day. What is the main risk?$qb$, $qb$Leaving out steps that have become automatic and invisible to you$qb$, $qb$Writing the instructions from your own habits rather than the correct method$qb$, $qb$Making the instructions too short for someone unfamiliar with the task$qb$, $qb$Using terms that a new person would not recognise or understand$qb$, $qb$Spending longer than the task deserves on writing it all down$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-026$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An email confirms an agreement but does not say who is doing which part. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reply setting out who does what, and ask them to confirm it is right$qb$, $qb$Reply asking them to clarify which parts each side is responsible for$qb$, $qb$Reply with your own understanding of the split of responsibilities$qb$, $qb$Proceed on your understanding and raise it if a problem appears$qb$, $qb$Proceed, since the agreement itself is confirmed and that is the main thing$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-027$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to record a measurement of 4.5. The form has no unit column. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Write the unit beside the figure, and ask for a unit column to be added$qb$, $qb$Write the unit beside the figure so the reading cannot be misread$qb$, $qb$Write the figure and note the unit separately in the comments$qb$, $qb$Write the figure, since everyone using the form knows the unit$qb$, $qb$Write the figure, since the form was designed by someone who knew better$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-028$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A written report you produced last month is being used to make a decision today. Some of the figures have since changed. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the decision-makers which figures have moved and what they are now$qb$, $qb$Tell them the report is a month old and some figures have changed$qb$, $qb$Send an updated version of the report with the new figures in it$qb$, $qb$Point out that the report was accurate at the time it was written$qb$, $qb$Say nothing, since the report was correct on the date it was issued$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-029$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are filling in a form and one field does not apply to your situation. What is best?$qb$, $qb$Mark it as not applicable, and say briefly why it does not apply$qb$, $qb$Mark it as not applicable, so nobody reads the gap as an oversight$qb$, $qb$Leave it blank, since the field does not apply to this case$qb$, $qb$Write the nearest thing that does apply, so the field is filled$qb$, $qb$Leave it blank and explain if anyone asks about it later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-030$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two team members keep separate records of the same stock. The numbers rarely match. What is the best step?$qb$, $qb$Agree one record as the official one, and how the other is updated from it$qb$, $qb$Agree which of the two records the team should treat as correct$qb$, $qb$Compare the two records regularly to find where they go out of step$qb$, $qb$Ask both to be more careful when they record the stock movements$qb$, $qb$Use whichever record is nearer the physical stock on the day$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-031$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are writing a handover for someone covering your holiday. What matters most?$qb$, $qb$What they must do, when, and what to do when something goes wrong$qb$, $qb$What they must do and when each task needs to be completed$qb$, $qb$A full description of your role and the tasks it involves$qb$, $qb$The tasks that are hardest, since the routine ones are obvious$qb$, $qb$Your contact details, so they can ask you while you are away$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-032$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A written instruction says 'notify the manager if the reading is high'. What is missing?$qb$, $qb$The figure above which a reading counts as high$qb$, $qb$A definition of what 'high' means in this context$qb$, $qb$The method for notifying the manager when it happens$qb$, $qb$Which manager should be notified about the reading$qb$, $qb$How quickly the manager needs to be notified$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-033$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You spot a typing error in a document that has already been sent to a client. It changes a date from the fourth to the fourteenth. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Send a correction now, stating the right date and what the error was$qb$, $qb$Send a corrected version to the client straight away$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager so they can decide how to correct it$qb$, $qb$Mention it the next time you are in contact with the client$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since the client will probably notice the date is wrong$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-MCQ-034$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team keeps notes in a shared document that anyone can edit. Entries are getting overwritten. What is the best fix?$qb$, $qb$Give each entry a date and an author, so changes can be traced$qb$, $qb$Ask people to add new entries rather than editing existing ones$qb$, $qb$Keep a backup copy so overwritten entries can be recovered$qb$, $qb$Restrict editing to a smaller number of people$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to be more careful when editing the document$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-SJT-035$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You work in a pharmacy. A prescription is handwritten and one word could read as either of two medicine names. The prescribing doctor's surgery is open for another hour. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Phone the surgery now and confirm the medicine before doing anything else$qb$, $qb$Phone the surgery to confirm, and note on the record that you checked$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to read it, since a second opinion may settle which it is$qb$, $qb$Dispense the one that fits the patient's usual medication history$qb$, $qb$Dispense the more likely reading and flag it if the patient queries it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-SJT-036$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a support worker. Your written notes on a client are read by the next shift. Today the client seemed unsettled but you cannot say exactly why. How do you record it?$qb$, $qb$Write what you saw and heard, and state plainly that the cause is unknown$qb$, $qb$Write what you observed in factual terms, without offering a cause$qb$, $qb$Write that the client seemed unsettled and note the times it happened$qb$, $qb$Write your view of what probably caused it, so the next shift is prepared$qb$, $qb$Leave it out, since you cannot describe the cause with any certainty$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-SJT-037$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are handing over a job to a colleague. There is a workaround you use for a known fault that is not written down anywhere. How do you handle the handover?$qb$, $qb$Write the workaround down, and record the fault it exists to get around$qb$, $qb$Write the workaround into the handover notes so they can follow it$qb$, $qb$Show them the workaround in person so they see how it is done$qb$, $qb$Tell them about it verbally and let them make their own notes$qb$, $qb$Leave it out, since they will work it out once they hit the fault$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-SJT-038$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to sign a delivery note for goods you have not counted. The driver is waiting and says he is running late. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Count the goods before signing, and write on the note what you actually checked$qb$, $qb$Count what you can and write on the note which items you verified$qb$, $qb$Ask the driver to wait while you complete the full count$qb$, $qb$Sign it and count afterwards, raising any shortfall with the supplier$qb$, $qb$Sign it unchecked, since the driver is late and deliveries are usually correct$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-SJT-039$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You keep a maintenance log. A machine failed last month and you fixed it, but you never wrote the entry. The machine has now failed again. How do you handle the log?$qb$, $qb$Record both failures now, and mark clearly which entry was written late$qb$, $qb$Record both failures now, with the correct dates for each of them$qb$, $qb$Record today's failure and mention the earlier one in the notes$qb$, $qb$Record today's failure only, since the earlier one is now in the past$qb$, $qb$Record today's failure and backdate the earlier entry to when it happened$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-SJT-040$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to witness a document by signing that you saw something happen. You arrived after it happened. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Decline, and offer to write down what you did see and when you arrived$qb$, $qb$Decline to sign, since you did not witness the event yourself$qb$, $qb$Sign, but write on it that you arrived after the event took place$qb$, $qb$Sign it, since your colleague was there and has described it to you$qb$, $qb$Sign it, since a witness signature is a formality in most cases$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-SJT-041$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A written procedure your team follows runs to eleven pages. Staff say it is too long to use, and several skip straight to the part they need. You are asked to shorten it. How do you proceed?$qb$, $qb$Find out which steps staff actually skip, and rebuild the order around how it is used$qb$, $qb$Shorten the wording throughout, but keep every step that is currently in it$qb$, $qb$Ask staff which parts they find hardest to follow while doing the work$qb$, $qb$Cut the detail as asked, since a procedure nobody reads helps nobody$qb$, $qb$Leave it as it is, since every step was put in there for a reason$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-SJT-042$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are taking notes at a meeting. Two people give different figures for the same thing and nobody resolves it during the discussion. How do you write it up?$qb$, $qb$Record both figures with who gave each, and put resolving it on the actions list$qb$, $qb$Record both figures with who gave each, and note it was left unresolved$qb$, $qb$Record the figure that seemed more reliable and note the disagreement$qb$, $qb$Record the figure given by the more senior person present$qb$, $qb$Leave both out, since an unresolved figure would confuse readers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-SJT-043$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are completing a checklist at the end of a shift. One check was not done because the area was locked. The box only offers yes or no. How do you complete it?$qb$, $qb$Mark it not done, and write beside it that the area was locked$qb$, $qb$Mark it as not done, since it was not carried out$qb$, $qb$Leave the box blank and raise the locked area with your supervisor$qb$, $qb$Mark it done, since the area was locked and could not have a problem$qb$, $qb$Mark it done, since being unable to access it is not your fault$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PWD-SJT-044$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You inherit a filing system where documents are named by the author's initials and a number. You cannot tell what any of them contain. How do you approach this?$qb$, $qb$Agree a naming rule with the team, then rename as documents are used$qb$, $qb$Propose a naming rule that says what each document contains$qb$, $qb$Keep an index that lists what each existing file name refers to$qb$, $qb$Rename everything yourself so the files make sense to you$qb$, $qb$Learn the existing system, since changing it would disrupt other people$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
