  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-022$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your shop is expecting its busiest week of the year in a month's time. What is the best preparation?$qb$, $qb$Work out what ran short last year and order against those figures now$qb$, $qb$Increase stock and staffing ahead of the week based on last year$qb$, $qb$Check with the team what they remember causing problems last year$qb$, $qb$Order extra stock across the board so nothing can run out$qb$, $qb$Handle it as it comes, since every busy period is different$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-023$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are planning a move to a new office. What is the most useful thing to establish early?$qb$, $qb$Which parts of the business cannot stop, and how they keep running during the move$qb$, $qb$The date of the move, so everyone can plan their own work around it$qb$, $qb$What needs to be packed and who is responsible for each area$qb$, $qb$The layout of the new office so people know where they will sit$qb$, $qb$The cost of the move, since that will need approval before anything else$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-024$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A regular report you produce depends on data from another team, which often arrives late. What is the best plan?$qb$, $qb$Agree a date with them that leaves you time, and a fallback if it slips$qb$, $qb$Agree a date with the other team that gives you enough time to work$qb$, $qb$Ask them to send whatever they have by your deadline each time$qb$, $qb$Build extra time into your own schedule to absorb the late data$qb$, $qb$Chase them each month until the data arrives, as you do now$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-025$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are asked to plan a piece of work in an area you do not know well. Two people in the company have done similar work before. What should you do first?$qb$, $qb$Find someone who has done it before and ask what usually goes wrong$qb$, $qb$Draft a plan and ask an experienced colleague to review it before you start$qb$, $qb$Find someone who has done it before and ask how they approached it$qb$, $qb$Read whatever written guidance exists on how this work is normally done$qb$, $qb$Start the work and build the plan as you learn what is actually involved$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-026$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team's plan assumes a new system will be live in March. What should the plan also contain?$qb$, $qb$What the team does from March if the system is not yet live$qb$, $qb$A check on how likely the March date is to be met$qb$, $qb$A note that the March date is an assumption, not a certainty$qb$, $qb$A request to the system team to confirm the March date$qb$, $qb$Nothing further, since the March date has been given officially$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-027$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have been asked to grow your team's output by a quarter next year. What should you plan first?$qb$, $qb$Which part of the current process will break first as volume rises$qb$, $qb$How much extra staffing and equipment the increase would need$qb$, $qb$Whether the current process can handle a quarter more work$qb$, $qb$How to get the team working faster to absorb the extra volume$qb$, $qb$How to recruit, since more output will need more people$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-028$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A contract you rely on comes up for renewal in eight months. What is the best time to act?$qb$, $qb$Now: find out the likely terms and line up at least one alternative supplier$qb$, $qb$Now: find out what the renewal terms are likely to be before they are set$qb$, $qb$About three months before, when the terms are likely to be clearer$qb$, $qb$When the supplier gets in touch with you about the renewal date$qb$, $qb$At renewal, since the current arrangement is working perfectly well$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-029$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are planning a project and your sponsor wants it finished in half the usual time. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Show what would have to be dropped or added to make that date work$qb$, $qb$Explain what the usual time is spent on and where it could compress$qb$, $qb$Ask why the shorter timescale is needed, so the plan can reflect it$qb$, $qb$Agree the date and look for savings once the work is under way$qb$, $qb$Agree the date, since the sponsor understands the business need$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-030$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team keeps being pulled onto urgent work that was not planned. What is the best step?$qb$, $qb$Leave planned capacity free each week for the urgent work that always comes$qb$, $qb$Track how much urgent work arrives, so it can be planned for$qb$, $qb$Ask that urgent requests come through you rather than direct to the team$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to work faster on planned work to absorb the interruptions$qb$, $qb$Accept it, since urgent work by its nature cannot be planned$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-031$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are setting up a new process. When should you decide how it will be measured?$qb$, $qb$Before it starts, and build the data collection into the process itself$qb$, $qb$Before it starts, so everyone agrees what good performance will mean$qb$, $qb$Shortly after it starts, once you can see what data is actually available$qb$, $qb$After a few months, when there is enough data for it to be meaningful$qb$, $qb$When someone gets round to asking how the new process is performing$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-032$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A key member of your team has handed in their notice. They leave in a month. What comes first?$qb$, $qb$List what only they know, and set dates for handing each part over$qb$, $qb$Arrange a handover covering their main responsibilities$qb$, $qb$Start recruiting a replacement as quickly as possible$qb$, $qb$Ask them to write up their role before they leave$qb$, $qb$Redistribute their work among the team once they have gone$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-033$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are planning next year's budget. Last year you overspent in one area by a large amount. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Find why that area overran, and build the corrected figure into the new budget$qb$, $qb$Increase the budget for that area to reflect what was actually spent$qb$, $qb$Review last year's spending across all areas before setting the new figures$qb$, $qb$Set the same budget again and manage that area more closely this time$qb$, $qb$Reduce that area's budget so the overspend cannot happen again$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-MCQ-034$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two projects you run will both need the same specialist in April. What should you do now?$qb$, $qb$Agree now which project gets April, and reschedule the other around it$qb$, $qb$Tell both project leads that the specialist is needed twice in April$qb$, $qb$Ask the specialist whether they can cover both in the same month$qb$, $qb$Wait until April and prioritise whichever project is further behind$qb$, $qb$Assume it will work out, since April is still several months away$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-035$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You run a small bakery. Your only oven is eleven years old and has needed two repairs this year. A replacement takes six weeks to arrive. Christmas is your busiest period, three months away. How do you plan for this?$qb$, $qb$Order the replacement now, so it is installed and tested before the busy period$qb$, $qb$Get the oven serviced now and find out how quickly a replacement could arrive$qb$, $qb$Find out which repair firms could attend quickly if it fails in December$qb$, $qb$Set money aside for a replacement in case the oven fails during December$qb$, $qb$Wait and see, since the oven is still working and repairs have been possible$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-036$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You manage a care home. Two of your five night staff have told you they are leaving in six weeks. Recruiting and training a night worker usually takes eight weeks. How do you plan?$qb$, $qb$Start recruiting now and arrange cover for the two-week gap you cannot avoid$qb$, $qb$Start recruiting immediately, since the timescales are already too tight$qb$, $qb$Ask the two leavers whether they could stay an extra fortnight$qb$, $qb$Cover the gap with agency staff once the two members have gone$qb$, $qb$Begin recruiting once they have left, so you know exactly what you need$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-037$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a teacher planning a school trip in two months. The coach, the venue and the parental consent forms all need arranging. How do you sequence this?$qb$, $qb$Book the coach and venue first, since those are the ones that can be taken$qb$, $qb$Book the coach and venue, then send the consent forms out to parents$qb$, $qb$Send the consent forms first, so you know how many places you need$qb$, $qb$Arrange all three at the same time so nothing is left to the end$qb$, $qb$Start with the consent forms, since parents take the longest to respond$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-038$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You supervise a warehouse. From January a new client will double the number of orders you send out. It is October, and your shelves are already almost full. How do you prepare?$qb$, $qb$Work out how much shelf space January needs, and add it before the orders start$qb$, $qb$Work out how much extra space and how many extra staff will be needed$qb$, $qb$Ask the client for a clearer estimate of what January will actually bring$qb$, $qb$Add staff in January and see how the shelves you already have cope$qb$, $qb$Handle it from January, since the real requirement will be clearer by then$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-039$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You lead a small IT team. The system you support has one server with no backup. It has run without failure for four years. How do you handle this?$qb$, $qb$Put a backup in place now, and test that it actually works before you need it$qb$, $qb$Put a backup server in place before there is any sign of trouble$qb$, $qb$Work out how long the business could operate if the server failed$qb$, $qb$Arrange a support contract so a failure would be repaired quickly$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since four years without failure suggests the server is reliable$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-040$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are opening a second branch in five months. You have never opened one before. How do you approach the planning?$qb$, $qb$Work out which items take longest to arrange, and start on those first$qb$, $qb$List everything that needs doing and put a date against each item$qb$, $qb$Ask someone who has opened a branch before how they went about it$qb$, $qb$Work through the tasks in order and adjust the plan as you go along$qb$, $qb$Start with the things you know, and deal with the rest as they come up$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-041$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You manage a team of eight. Your busiest month is always September, and every year the team works late through it. It is now May. How do you plan differently this year?$qb$, $qb$Work out what September actually needs, then move what can be done in summer$qb$, $qb$Work out how much extra capacity September needs and plan for it now$qb$, $qb$Ask the team what made last September hardest for them$qb$, $qb$Arrange extra staff for September so the workload is spread$qb$, $qb$Plan as usual and accept that September is always a difficult month$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-042$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A grant that funds a third of your department's work ends in ten months. Reapplying takes about three months and is not guaranteed. How do you plan?$qb$, $qb$Start the reapplication now, and plan what the department does if it fails$qb$, $qb$Start the reapplication early so it is submitted in good time$qb$, $qb$Find out how likely the reapplication is to succeed before deciding$qb$, $qb$Look for other funding sources while the current grant still runs$qb$, $qb$Reapply when the process opens, since the current grant runs for ten months$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-043$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are planning a system change that will affect every department. You have been asked to have it live in three months. How do you begin?$qb$, $qb$Ask each department what would break for them, before fixing the sequence$qb$, $qb$Ask each department what they need from the change before you plan it$qb$, $qb$Draft a plan and circulate it to departments for their comments$qb$, $qb$Plan the technical work first, then tell departments what to expect$qb$, $qb$Start the work and involve departments as their part comes up$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-044$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You run a garage. The licence that lets your senior mechanic sign off vehicle fitness checks expires in four months. The renewal course takes two days, and places are usually booked up three months in advance. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Book the course now, and check whether anyone else should be trained as cover$qb$, $qb$Book the renewal course now, before the available places are taken$qb$, $qb$Check when the next available course dates are before booking$qb$, $qb$Book the course a month before the certificate runs out$qb$, $qb$Wait until nearer the time, since four months is plenty of notice$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-045$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team is asked to deliver a piece of work by a fixed date. Halfway through planning you see the date can only be met if nothing goes wrong at all. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Say so now, and offer either a later date or less work for the same date$qb$, $qb$Say the plan has no room for error and ask how they want to handle it$qb$, $qb$Add contingency time to the plan and present the later date that results$qb$, $qb$Present the plan as it is and manage the risks carefully as you go$qb$, $qb$Present the plan as it is, since the date was fixed before you were involved$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-046$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You manage a restaurant. Your head chef has asked for three weeks off in July, your busiest month. You have four months' notice. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Agree it, and have the cover run the kitchen one weekend while the chef is there$qb$, $qb$Agree the leave and arrange the cover well before July comes around$qb$, $qb$Ask whether the three weeks could be split or moved across to June$qb$, $qb$Agree the leave and arrange agency cover nearer to the time itself$qb$, $qb$Ask the chef to take the leave outside the restaurant's busiest month$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-047$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are planning a fundraising event. It depends on a well-known speaker who has verbally agreed but not confirmed in writing. The event is in four months. How do you proceed?$qb$, $qb$Get it confirmed in writing now, and decide what the event becomes without them$qb$, $qb$Get the agreement confirmed in writing before anything else is booked$qb$, $qb$Ask the speaker to confirm and continue planning around their attendance$qb$, $qb$Continue planning and confirm the speaker closer to the event date$qb$, $qb$Continue planning, since a verbal agreement from a professional is reliable$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-048$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your organisation is introducing a new rule that all staff must complete online training within three months. You manage twelve people across three shifts. How do you plan it?$qb$, $qb$Schedule slots across the three months so no shift is short-staffed at once$qb$, $qb$Schedule the training across the three months rather than leaving it late$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the deadline and ask them to fit it in around their work$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to complete it in the first month so it is out of the way$qb$, $qb$Remind the team as the deadline approaches and let them arrange it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-049$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are taking over a project from a colleague who leaves in two weeks. The project files and status reports are up to date. The project runs for another year. How do you use the two weeks?$qb$, $qb$Go through what is coming up in the next six months and what it depends on$qb$, $qb$Go through the project's current position and the immediate next steps$qb$, $qb$Ask them to write a full handover document before they leave$qb$, $qb$Work alongside them for two weeks and learn the project as you go$qb$, $qb$Read the project files, since the documentation should cover what you need$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-PSP-SJT-050$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your company is planning to launch a product in six months. Nobody has yet asked what happens if it sells far better than expected. How do you raise this?$qb$, $qb$Ask what the plan is if demand doubles, and where supply would break first$qb$, $qb$Ask whether the plan covers demand being higher than the forecast$qb$, $qb$Ask what the forecast is based on and how confident people are in it$qb$, $qb$Raise it as a risk to be reviewed once early sales figures arrive$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since planning for unexpected success is a good problem to have$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
