  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-MCQ-026$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague asked you two months ago to update a customer list. It takes about half a day and has no deadline. It has been on your weekly list ever since and has never been done. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Either book the half day this week or tell your colleague it will not be done$qb$, $qb$Ask your colleague whether the update is still needed before doing anything$qb$, $qb$Move it to the top of the list so that it gets done sometime this week$qb$, $qb$Do part of it now and fit the rest in whenever some spare time appears$qb$, $qb$Keep carrying it over, since something more urgent always comes up$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-MCQ-027$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are handing unfinished work to a colleague while you go on leave. What matters most?$qb$, $qb$What is done, what is left, and anything only you currently know$qb$, $qb$What is done and what still needs to be completed$qb$, $qb$A written summary of the work and where it has reached$qb$, $qb$Your contact details so they can ask you while you are away$qb$, $qb$A note of the deadline so they know when it must be finished$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-MCQ-028$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team finishes projects but rarely reviews them. What is the main cost of this?$qb$, $qb$The same avoidable problems appear in the next project$qb$, $qb$Nothing is learned from what went well or badly$qb$, $qb$There is no record of how the project actually went$qb$, $qb$The team does not get recognition for finishing the work$qb$, $qb$There is no cost, since the projects are being delivered$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-MCQ-029$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A supplier says your order is 'in progress'. It was due three days ago. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what remains and for a date, and say what happens if it slips again$qb$, $qb$Ask what is left to do and when they now expect to deliver$qb$, $qb$Ask them to confirm the new delivery date in writing$qb$, $qb$Wait a few more days, since 'in progress' means it is moving$qb$, $qb$Cancel the order, since they have already missed the date$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-MCQ-030$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are asked to confirm a task is finished, but one small part is outstanding. What should you say?$qb$, $qb$That it is not finished, name the part outstanding, and give a date$qb$, $qb$That one part is outstanding and when you expect it to be done$qb$, $qb$That it is nearly finished and you will confirm shortly$qb$, $qb$That it is finished, since the outstanding part is very small$qb$, $qb$That it is finished, and complete the last part before anyone notices$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-MCQ-031$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team's work regularly stalls at the approval step. What is the best first action?$qb$, $qb$Find out how long approvals take, and build that into when work starts$qb$, $qb$Find out what causes approvals to take as long as they do$qb$, $qb$Ask the approver whether the process can be made quicker$qb$, $qb$Submit work for approval earlier so there is more time$qb$, $qb$Accept it, since approvals are outside your team's control$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-MCQ-032$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realise a task you completed last month was done to the wrong specification. Nobody has noticed. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Raise it now with what was wrong and what putting it right would involve$qb$, $qb$Raise it now and explain how the wrong specification was used$qb$, $qb$Work out how much difference it actually makes before raising it$qb$, $qb$Correct it quietly, since nobody has noticed and the work is done$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since raising a month-old error now would help nobody$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-MCQ-033$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A piece of work is finished but the customer has not confirmed they are satisfied. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them directly to confirm, and say what you will do if something is wrong$qb$, $qb$Ask them to confirm that the work meets what they asked for$qb$, $qb$Send a message saying the work is complete and await their reply$qb$, $qb$Treat it as closed, since the work has been delivered to them$qb$, $qb$Treat it as closed and deal with any complaint if one arrives$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-MCQ-034$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are close to finishing a job when a more interesting piece of work arrives. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Finish the current job first, since restarting it later costs more time$qb$, $qb$Finish the current job before taking on the new one$qb$, $qb$Ask whether the new work can wait until you have finished$qb$, $qb$Start the new work and return to the old job afterwards$qb$, $qb$Switch to the new work, since it is more valuable to the business$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-035$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You work in a tailoring shop. A customer's alterations were finished four days ago but the garment is still on the rack because nobody phoned to say it was ready. The customer has now arrived asking about it. How do you handle this?$qb$, $qb$Apologise, hand it over, and set up a way that finished work gets phoned through$qb$, $qb$Apologise, hand over the garment, and explain that the call was missed$qb$, $qb$Hand over the garment and apologise for the delay in contacting them$qb$, $qb$Hand over the garment and explain that the shop has been very busy$qb$, $qb$Hand over the garment without mentioning that it was ready four days ago$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-036$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a plumber. You have fixed a leak but the customer is out and cannot check the work. You have another job to get to. How do you close this one off?$qb$, $qb$Leave a note of what you did and what to watch for, and ring later to confirm$qb$, $qb$Ring the customer later to say what you did and check the repair is holding$qb$, $qb$Leave a note describing the repair you have carried out today$qb$, $qb$Leave the site tidy and assume the customer will call if anything is wrong$qb$, $qb$Leave for the next job, since the work itself has been completed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-037$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You lead a small team. A project finished two weeks ago but nobody has told the finance team, so it is still showing as open and staff time is still being charged to it. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Tell finance today, and add closing the code to the steps for finishing a project$qb$, $qb$Tell finance today so the project can be closed and charging stops$qb$, $qb$Tell finance and check whether any other finished projects are still open$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to stop charging time to it and tell finance later this week$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since finance will close it when they review open projects$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-038$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a nurse finishing a shift. You started a dressing change but were called away, and the patient is now half-dressed and waiting. Your shift ends in five minutes. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Finish it yourself, then record what was done before you leave$qb$, $qb$Ask someone on the next shift to finish it and show them where you got to$qb$, $qb$Record where you got to and let the next shift complete the dressing$qb$, $qb$Tell the next shift on your way out that a dressing was left part done$qb$, $qb$Leave it for the next shift, since your shift is about to finish$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-039$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a graphic designer. A client approved a design three weeks ago, but you never sent the final files because you were waiting for one last detail that never arrived. How do you act?$qb$, $qb$Send what is ready today, say what is outstanding, and give a date for it$qb$, $qb$Contact the client, explain the hold-up, and ask how they want to proceed$qb$, $qb$Chase the missing detail again before sending the client anything$qb$, $qb$Wait for the detail, since sending incomplete files would look unprofessional$qb$, $qb$Wait until the client asks, since they have not chased you about it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-040$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You manage a small shop. Your team is good at starting improvements but three of the last four have been abandoned halfway. How do you address this?$qb$, $qb$Finish one before starting another, and agree what 'finished' means for each$qb$, $qb$Agree that no new improvement starts until the current one is finished$qb$, $qb$Ask the team why the improvements keep being abandoned halfway$qb$, $qb$Set deadlines for each improvement so they are completed on time$qb$, $qb$Start fewer improvements, since the team clearly cannot sustain them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-041$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a mechanic. A car you repaired last week has come back with the same fault. You are fairly sure you fixed it correctly. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Check what you actually did against the fault now, before deciding anything$qb$, $qb$Look at the car again to see whether the fault is the same one$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer exactly what the car is doing and when it started$qb$, $qb$Redo the repair, since the fault returning suggests it did not hold$qb$, $qb$Explain that the repair was done correctly and something else must be wrong$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-042$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are an office administrator. Every month you produce a report, send it out, and hear nothing. This month you notice a serious error in last month's version. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Send a correction now, say what changed, and ask who acted on the old figures$qb$, $qb$Send a corrected version now and explain what the error was$qb$, $qb$Send a correction and check whether earlier months had the same error$qb$, $qb$Correct it in this month's report without drawing attention to it$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since nobody responded to last month's report anyway$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-043$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You run a small building firm. A job is complete except for one door handle that is on order. The customer wants to make the final payment and close the job. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Agree a date for the handle in writing, and settle the payment against that$qb$, $qb$Explain the handle is outstanding and agree when it will be fitted$qb$, $qb$Take the payment and fit the handle when it arrives from the supplier$qb$, $qb$Take the full payment, since the job is finished apart from one small item$qb$, $qb$Ask them to wait until the handle is fitted before paying anything$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-044$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a warehouse team leader. Stock counts get started each month but are rarely completed, so the figures are never fully reliable. How do you address it?$qb$, $qb$Split the count into sections with a date each, so each part actually closes$qb$, $qb$Set a completion date for the count and hold the team to finishing it$qb$, $qb$Find out at what point in the count the team usually stops$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to work faster so the count is finished each month$qb$, $qb$Accept partial counts, since a rough figure is better than nothing$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-045$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a trainer. You ran a two-day course last month. Nobody has asked for feedback and you are due to run it again next week. How do you handle this?$qb$, $qb$Ask the last group what did not work, and change the course before running it$qb$, $qb$Note your own observations from last time and change the course accordingly$qb$, $qb$Ask the previous group for feedback to use after the next course runs$qb$, $qb$Run it the same way and gather feedback at the end this time$qb$, $qb$Run it as before, since nobody raised any problems with the last one$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-046$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are an accounts clerk. A supplier invoice has been sitting in a query folder for six weeks. Nobody has chased it and you do not know who raised the query. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Find out who raised it and what is needed, and set a date to resolve it$qb$, $qb$Contact the supplier to find out whether the query still stands$qb$, $qb$Find out who raised the query and pass the details across to them$qb$, $qb$Pass it to your manager, since the query is not yours to resolve$qb$, $qb$Leave it in the folder, since nobody has chased it in six weeks$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-047$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a teacher. You set your class a project four weeks ago and have marked none of it. The students keep asking. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Tell them a date you will return it by, and mark against that date$qb$, $qb$Tell the class when they can expect their marked work back$qb$, $qb$Start marking now and return the work as each piece is finished$qb$, $qb$Explain that you have been busy and will get to it when you can$qb$, $qb$Return the work unmarked, since four weeks makes the marks less useful$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-048$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a project coordinator. Your project ends on Friday. Three small items will not be done and nobody has mentioned them. How do you close the project?$qb$, $qb$List the three items with who picks them up, and close the rest formally$qb$, $qb$List the three outstanding items in the closing report for the project$qb$, $qb$Raise the three items with the sponsor before the project closes$qb$, $qb$Close the project on Friday and finish the three items afterwards$qb$, $qb$Close the project on Friday, since three small items will not be missed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-049$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You work on a helpdesk. You resolved a caller's problem but did not close the ticket, and it has now been reassigned to a colleague who is starting again. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Tell your colleague now, close the ticket, and record what the fix was$qb$, $qb$Tell your colleague straight away so they stop working on it$qb$, $qb$Close the ticket now and explain the delay if anyone asks about it$qb$, $qb$Let your colleague continue, since they have already started on it$qb$, $qb$Say nothing, since the caller's problem has been solved either way$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ECF-SJT-050$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a shift manager. Your team completes its work but leaves the area untidy, so the next shift spends the first twenty minutes clearing up. How do you address this?$qb$, $qb$Make clearing the area part of finishing the shift, and check it before leaving$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to clear the area before the end of each shift$qb$, $qb$Raise it with the team and ask what makes clearing up difficult$qb$, $qb$Ask the next shift to raise it directly with your team when it happens$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since the work itself is being completed on time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
